WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.250 --> 00:00:09.870
We’re gonna review a proposition.

00:00:09.870 --> 00:00:15.490
Now, if I ever tell you something in enough
detail that you've already heard that you’ve

00:00:15.490 --> 00:00:21.630
heard it enough, please tell me because I
can’t precisely remember what I told you.

00:00:21.630 --> 00:00:27.000
I know the line of the narrative throughout
the entire course, and we will return to some

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:32.480
things fairly frequently, but I don't want
to tell you the same story three times, unless

00:00:32.480 --> 00:00:35.600
you’re particularly compelled by that story.

00:00:35.600 --> 00:00:38.080
So let me know.

00:00:38.080 --> 00:00:44.520
We’re going to start with a proposition,
and the proposition is that in order to orient

00:00:44.520 --> 00:00:50.630
yourself properly in life, there's two classes
of information you have to have access to.

00:00:50.630 --> 00:00:56.530
One class of information is information about
the nature of the objective world, and the

00:00:56.530 --> 00:01:01.789
objective world is described in a manner - or
the world when it is treated objectively - is

00:01:01.789 --> 00:01:06.070
described in a manner that strips all the
subjectivity from it.

00:01:06.070 --> 00:01:08.100
That’s the technology.

00:01:08.100 --> 00:01:14.590
The philosophical technology that's behind
the Scientific Revolution - involves stripping

00:01:14.590 --> 00:01:22.980
the subjectivity from the sense mediated consequences
of certain kinds of actions because when you

00:01:22.980 --> 00:01:26.720
lay out an experiment, you're not just talking
about what happened, you're talking about

00:01:26.720 --> 00:01:29.020
what you did and then what happened.

00:01:29.020 --> 00:01:32.700
And you're supposed to be able to do it in
a way that ensures that if someone else followed

00:01:32.700 --> 00:01:36.700
the exact same procedure, they would obviously
observe the same consequences.

00:01:36.700 --> 00:01:43.840
And, in some sense, we’re trying to establish
a realm of knowledge that is universally applicable

00:01:43.840 --> 00:01:46.740
to people despite their individual differences.

00:01:46.740 --> 00:01:50.160
That's one way of thinking about it, and obviously
that works.

00:01:50.160 --> 00:01:56.060
Whether or not it works in a Darwinian sense,
we still have to determine because it could

00:01:56.060 --> 00:02:03.231
easily be that our development of the scientific
method is what will make us go extinct, under

00:02:03.231 --> 00:02:06.759
which circumstances you might consider it
to have been an error.

00:02:06.759 --> 00:02:14.570
But it doesn't really matter at the moment
because so far so good, roughly speaking.

00:02:14.570 --> 00:02:19.310
Now, people think the world is an objective
place because we can describe it objectively,

00:02:19.310 --> 00:02:25.030
and that's a much more debatable presupposition.

00:02:25.030 --> 00:02:31.480
It’s partly debatable because it's actually
based on a decision in some sense, rather

00:02:31.480 --> 00:02:38.450
than evidence, partly because your knowledge
is finite in every direction in some sense.

00:02:38.450 --> 00:02:43.370
So what that means in a way is no matter what
you know about anything, you don't know everything

00:02:43.370 --> 00:02:44.620
about that thing.

00:02:44.620 --> 00:02:47.350
And so, you kind of ground out in ignorance
at some point.

00:02:47.350 --> 00:02:51.670
So what means is you that you have to buttress
your thinking with a set of assumptions that

00:02:51.670 --> 00:02:55.600
you don't question or you just end up in an
infinite regress.

00:02:55.600 --> 00:03:02.660
One of those sets of assumptions is usually
reality definition sets of assumptions, or

00:03:02.660 --> 00:03:07.379
truth definition sets of assumptions, but
the thing is generally no matter what your

00:03:07.379 --> 00:03:12.150
theory of reality is, or what your theory
of truth is, there are alternative theories

00:03:12.150 --> 00:03:17.379
of reality and alternative theories of truth
that are equally rigorous and that have different

00:03:17.379 --> 00:03:19.370
domains of application.

00:03:19.370 --> 00:03:30.629
And it’s a very tricky business, so for
example, if you're a reductionist materialist,

00:03:30.629 --> 00:03:39.340
you're going to make the presupposition that
brain physiology is more real than emotional

00:03:39.340 --> 00:03:40.370
experience.

00:03:40.370 --> 00:03:45.549
I suppose that's in some sense because you
can't get access to someone else’s emotional

00:03:45.549 --> 00:03:50.150
experiences, at least not directly whereas
you can get universal access to the fundamental

00:03:50.150 --> 00:03:51.840
details of someone’s brain physiology.

00:03:51.840 --> 00:03:53.490
It’s something like that.

00:03:53.490 --> 00:04:00.110
Anyways now, that's still an arbitrary assumption
because the person and you as well are going

00:04:00.110 --> 00:04:04.760
to be acting all the time as if your emotions
and motivational states are real and so then

00:04:04.760 --> 00:04:09.379
you run into a problem and the problem is
well, how do you know what someone actually

00:04:09.379 --> 00:04:10.379
believes?

00:04:10.379 --> 00:04:16.650
You listen to what they say or do you listen
to their explicit and well articulated theories,

00:04:16.650 --> 00:04:18.400
or do you watch how they act?

00:04:18.400 --> 00:04:26.830
And the answer to that is, you make your choice
and generally there are reasons for that.

00:04:26.830 --> 00:04:35.870
Now, one of the conceptual tools that I have
found helpful in my attempts to avoid confusion

00:04:35.870 --> 00:04:39.310
when dealing with the fact that there are
very many ways of looking at the world is

00:04:39.310 --> 00:04:46.720
to start thinking about theories of reality
as tools rather than descriptions and I find

00:04:46.720 --> 00:04:50.400
that extraordinarily useful as a metaphor.

00:04:50.400 --> 00:04:54.960
If you have a toolbox full of tools, you don't
necessarily try to reduce the utility of one

00:04:54.960 --> 00:04:59.160
tool to another and you don't actually complain
that all tools aren't the same.

00:04:59.160 --> 00:05:05.199
A wrench isn't a very good hammer, but that
doesn't mean you can criticize it on that

00:05:05.199 --> 00:05:06.199
grounds.

00:05:06.199 --> 00:05:11.240
You might not want to use it as a hammer,
although you could I suppose, but it’s not

00:05:11.240 --> 00:05:15.061
a justifiable criticism, and it looks to me
- and this is something that a psychologist

00:05:15.061 --> 00:05:19.000
named George Kelly pointed out as well, although
I think it’s also an element of pragmatic

00:05:19.000 --> 00:05:26.090
philosophy is that one set of tools for one
thing, and another set of tools for another

00:05:26.090 --> 00:05:27.480
thing.

00:05:27.480 --> 00:05:33.040
That’s helpful, that’s helpful.

00:05:33.040 --> 00:05:36.699
And then you are also thinking about the sorts
of problems that you might solve with different

00:05:36.699 --> 00:05:41.960
forms of tools, and one problem is what are
things made of - in an objective sense let’s

00:05:41.960 --> 00:05:42.960
say.

00:05:42.960 --> 00:05:48.979
And another problem might be what is the landscape
of experience, and that's more of a phenomenological

00:05:48.979 --> 00:05:49.979
problem.

00:05:49.979 --> 00:05:52.150
That’s the problem the phenomenologists
set out, and then the other problem might

00:05:52.150 --> 00:05:58.220
be how do you, and should you conduct yourself.

00:05:58.220 --> 00:06:03.699
And it could easily be that you can't use
the same tool to solve all of those problems,

00:06:03.699 --> 00:06:09.310
and I would say particularly true, technically,
when you're dealing with science because science

00:06:09.310 --> 00:06:14.479
in its very nature refuses to answer questions
about what it is that you should do, apart

00:06:14.479 --> 00:06:18.919
from some implicit assumption that you should
do science which is actually not -

00:06:18.919 --> 00:06:24.770
Well that’s actually not a scientific assumption,
by the way, because it’s a moral assumption,

00:06:24.770 --> 00:06:30.300
and the moral assumption is that if you conduct
yourself in a scientific manner, things will

00:06:30.300 --> 00:06:34.970
be better because why would you run around
doing science to make things worse, although

00:06:34.970 --> 00:06:37.319
you absolutely can.

00:06:37.319 --> 00:06:43.210
And, we perhaps are, and even if we aren’t,
we still might.

00:06:43.210 --> 00:06:47.819
So that's one of the things I find quite interesting
about the scientific endeavor, and this is

00:06:47.819 --> 00:06:52.090
something Jung observed was that it had to
be nested in something outside of itself to

00:06:52.090 --> 00:06:53.130
be given its motive force.

00:06:53.130 --> 00:06:58.669
It had to be nested in a system of moral assumptions,
and the fundamental moral assumption is the

00:06:58.669 --> 00:07:02.819
world will be a better place if we conduct
ourselves along scientific lines, because

00:07:02.819 --> 00:07:04.240
why do it?

00:07:04.240 --> 00:07:08.690
And that's a statement that cannot be analyzed
from within the confines of the scientific

00:07:08.690 --> 00:07:12.100
worldview because it doesn't analyze those
sorts of questions.

00:07:12.100 --> 00:07:17.500
Now, you can say they're not real, but that's
a stupid answer because - I think it’s a

00:07:17.500 --> 00:07:20.440
cop out, maybe it’s not a stupid answers,
but it probably is.

00:07:20.440 --> 00:07:26.110
At least it’s a cop out because if you don't
take that question seriously, you can't really

00:07:26.110 --> 00:07:28.440
justify engaging in the activity.

00:07:28.440 --> 00:07:32.020
So you could refuse to answer it but you're
basically answering it by engaging in the

00:07:32.020 --> 00:07:39.789
activity anyways so even if you're not saying
it, you're still justifying it.

00:07:39.789 --> 00:07:43.310
Assuming you can draw conclusions from people’s
actions like you can from their statements

00:07:43.310 --> 00:07:46.120
- and it’s hard to really dispute that I
would say.

00:07:46.120 --> 00:07:51.230
If you're wise, in fact, I think you derive
more conclusions from their actions than their

00:07:51.230 --> 00:07:52.620
statements.

00:07:52.620 --> 00:08:01.229
So that’s the underbelly of the first proposition,
and the development of that is something like

00:08:01.229 --> 00:08:08.710
the means by which information is transmitted
that is relevant to the domain of behavior

00:08:08.710 --> 00:08:13.970
is not the same means by which it’s transmitted
- by which scientific information is transmitted.

00:08:13.970 --> 00:08:20.199
And we kinda know that too, without any real
debate, I believe, because how old do you

00:08:20.199 --> 00:08:23.319
think science is as a technical endeavor?

00:08:23.319 --> 00:08:28.520
What would you estimate?

00:08:28.520 --> 00:08:32.079
Hundreds of years.

00:08:32.079 --> 00:08:35.740
Maybe thousands of years if you’re willing
to give the Greeks some credit, but really

00:08:35.740 --> 00:08:36.810
probably not.

00:08:36.810 --> 00:08:42.560
Probably hundreds because Aristotle wasn't
a scientist - like he wasn't a bad observer,

00:08:42.560 --> 00:08:46.790
but he certainly wasn't an empirical observer,
and no one got science straightened out till

00:08:46.790 --> 00:08:51.970
what - Newton, Descartes, Bacon, and you know
there's a handful of other characters who

00:08:51.970 --> 00:08:54.050
were thinking around that period of time.

00:08:54.050 --> 00:09:00.380
They sort of established the proper methods,
really, and also the philosophy that underlies

00:09:00.380 --> 00:09:01.380
it.

00:09:01.380 --> 00:09:02.380
It’s not very old.

00:09:02.380 --> 00:09:03.380
Six hundred years, you know, five hundred
years.

00:09:03.380 --> 00:09:04.380
That’s really nothing.

00:09:04.380 --> 00:09:07.230
It’s the blink of an eye.

00:09:07.230 --> 00:09:08.910
It’s yesterday, if that.

00:09:08.910 --> 00:09:11.780
It might even be an hour ago.

00:09:11.780 --> 00:09:17.422
It’s nothing, but obviously people were
transmitting information about how to act

00:09:17.422 --> 00:09:24.280
to each other for untold millennia before
that, and it’s probably more like - it depends

00:09:24.280 --> 00:09:33.200
on what you're willing to consider transmission
of information, but even octopi couldn't imitate,

00:09:33.200 --> 00:09:34.670
and they're the smartest invertebrate.

00:09:34.670 --> 00:09:36.150
They’re quite bright, those things.

00:09:36.150 --> 00:09:41.649
They only live a couple of years so they can't
really get things going.

00:09:41.649 --> 00:09:42.649
They can problem solve.

00:09:42.649 --> 00:09:46.100
They can pull corks out of bottles to get
like a crab in a bottle, and if they watch

00:09:46.100 --> 00:09:50.320
an octopus - if an octopus watches another
octopus pull a cork out of a bottle to get

00:09:50.320 --> 00:09:56.390
a tasty crab, then that octopus knows how
to do it, which is pretty impressive.

00:09:56.390 --> 00:10:00.740
And you could kind of see why octopi can kind
of do that because not only do they have a

00:10:00.740 --> 00:10:04.550
brain, but they also have little tentacle-y
appendages which allow them to manipulate

00:10:04.550 --> 00:10:05.550
things.

00:10:05.550 --> 00:10:11.140
So, one of the precursors to being able to
be intelligent in any act of way is that you

00:10:11.140 --> 00:10:16.480
have to have tentacle-y appendages so you
can manipulate the world.

00:10:16.480 --> 00:10:19.970
At least for it to be anything resembling
what we would consider to be intelligence,

00:10:19.970 --> 00:10:23.820
and that's part of the reason dolphins aren't
really as intelligent as people like to think

00:10:23.820 --> 00:10:25.370
because what are they gonna do?

00:10:25.370 --> 00:10:27.710
They can't build cities with their flippers.

00:10:27.710 --> 00:10:33.070
They're kinda trapped in their test tube bodies,
and the same thing applies to whales.

00:10:33.070 --> 00:10:37.810
Obviously they have very large brains, and
they're very social, and they can learn, but

00:10:37.810 --> 00:10:41.700
whatever their intelligence is, it’s not
like our intelligence because we run around

00:10:41.700 --> 00:10:46.760
tearing things apart and putting them back
together and we’ve got the body for that.

00:10:46.760 --> 00:10:51.860
So anyways, long before we were practicing
science, we were doing perfectly well, in

00:10:51.860 --> 00:10:56.370
a sense, without any real knowledge of the
objective world at all - at least not any

00:10:56.370 --> 00:10:58.660
scientific knowledge about the objective world.

00:10:58.660 --> 00:11:03.649
So another thing you might observe about that
is that you can survive perfectly well without

00:11:03.649 --> 00:11:08.120
knowing any science at all, in an articulated
and developed manner.

00:11:08.120 --> 00:11:11.029
Of course animals are in that category.

00:11:11.029 --> 00:11:17.160
So, that also, I think in some sense, undermines
the claim of science to anything approaching

00:11:17.160 --> 00:11:21.750
a universal truth because obviously life can
get along perfectly well without it.

00:11:21.750 --> 00:11:24.341
Now it depends on some degree whether you’re
a Darwinian or a Newtonian.

00:11:24.341 --> 00:11:25.341
Perhaps the unwillingness to commence on what
people should do is one feature of modern

00:11:25.341 --> 00:11:46.770
science than it is of science in general because
I’m thinking someone like Clifford.

00:11:46.770 --> 00:11:49.950
I don’t know if you know his essay on belief.

00:11:49.950 --> 00:11:51.120
I don’t, no.

00:11:51.120 --> 00:11:56.600
Oh okay, well his maxim is that basically
it’s wrong always for anyone everywhere

00:11:56.600 --> 00:11:59.740
to believe anything on his push to evidence.

00:11:59.740 --> 00:12:02.250
And he sort of tries to prove it - he’s
a mathematician.

00:12:02.250 --> 00:12:04.510
He tries to prove it on a moral basis.

00:12:04.510 --> 00:12:05.760
Of course, it’s not scientific.

00:12:05.760 --> 00:12:10.779
That kind of claim can’t be scientific but
Yeah, well I mean obviously the people who

00:12:10.779 --> 00:12:15.029
began the Scientific Revolution thought of
it as part of a moral movement.

00:12:15.029 --> 00:12:16.740
I mean it was part of the enlightenment.

00:12:16.740 --> 00:12:23.029
It was part of bringing clarity to our conceptualizations
of the world and that was supposed to be a

00:12:23.029 --> 00:12:28.990
positive thing, but the only issue is that
claim seems to be outside of the claim on

00:12:28.990 --> 00:12:30.649
science, in some sense.

00:12:30.649 --> 00:12:33.649
Anyways, you get the picture with regard to
the argument.

00:12:33.649 --> 00:12:44.200
Okay, now the next step might be if you need
or can utilize or there exists information

00:12:44.200 --> 00:12:50.170
that is not strictly scientific, one might
be, well, where did that information come

00:12:50.170 --> 00:12:51.170
from?

00:12:51.170 --> 00:12:52.170
That's a good one.

00:12:52.170 --> 00:12:53.550
How is it transmitted?

00:12:53.550 --> 00:12:54.649
That's another one.

00:12:54.649 --> 00:12:58.860
And the worst of it is, of course, how might
it be verified?

00:12:58.860 --> 00:13:02.670
Because one of the issues - one of the things
that scientists have really got down, and

00:13:02.670 --> 00:13:07.471
it’s a major deal, is that they can lay
forth a theory of truth and they they can

00:13:07.471 --> 00:13:12.779
operate within that theory of truth and pretty
much everyone can agree on what it is.

00:13:12.779 --> 00:13:15.790
The more well established the science, the
more that’s the case.

00:13:15.790 --> 00:13:20.460
Like people don’t really doubt Einstein’s
- people really don’t doubt quantum theory

00:13:20.460 --> 00:13:24.560
anymore, and you guys don’t doubt it because
there you are using your computers and you

00:13:24.560 --> 00:13:29.029
wouldn’t be able to use your computers if
quantum theory wasn't right, so it’s certainly

00:13:29.029 --> 00:13:30.029
right enough.

00:13:30.029 --> 00:13:35.410
But enough more impressively than that, whenever
someone’s tried to prove it wrong, in any

00:13:35.410 --> 00:13:43.310
possible way, even in its most bizarre predictions
over the last hundred and some years, it’s

00:13:43.310 --> 00:13:45.680
passed every test with flying colors.

00:13:45.680 --> 00:13:50.210
So you know, you can make pretty damn solid
truth claims from within the scientific perspective,

00:13:50.210 --> 00:13:54.680
but the problem is that it’s a lot harder
to do that from within a moral perspective

00:13:54.680 --> 00:13:57.950
and there's all sorts of reasons for that.

00:13:57.950 --> 00:14:05.110
One reason might be that there's a certain
amount of situational complexity related to

00:14:05.110 --> 00:14:08.110
every moral question that you can't reduce.

00:14:08.110 --> 00:14:15.380
For example, imagine if you're a clinical
psychologist and you're listening to two people

00:14:15.380 --> 00:14:22.209
who know each other very well, maybe husband
and wife, discuss their last argument.

00:14:22.209 --> 00:14:26.649
And you might say - well you might think - well
let's get to the bottom of this.

00:14:26.649 --> 00:14:29.560
Let's find out what this argument is about.

00:14:29.560 --> 00:14:35.279
You might think, if you were scientifically
inclined, that it is in fact about something

00:14:35.279 --> 00:14:40.089
which means that if you investigated long
enough, you can actually figure out what the

00:14:40.089 --> 00:14:44.410
facts are, but I actually don't believe that's
true.

00:14:44.410 --> 00:14:47.240
And the reason I don’t think it’s true
is this.

00:14:47.240 --> 00:14:50.769
Imagine you have a - there’s this old joke
about these two friends.

00:14:50.769 --> 00:14:55.860
They're like 85 years old and they're sitting
on the park bench feeding the pigeons, and

00:14:55.860 --> 00:15:01.279
one friend says to the other “64,” and
the other guy cracks up and laughs like mad

00:15:01.279 --> 00:15:02.279
about that.

00:15:02.279 --> 00:15:06.170
Then the other guy says “83,” then the
other guy cracks up and laughs about that,

00:15:06.170 --> 00:15:09.730
and some guy’s watching them and wondering
what in the world is going on.

00:15:09.730 --> 00:15:13.790
So the person comes over to these old guys
and says what’s the scoop here?

00:15:13.790 --> 00:15:17.880
All you’re doing is sitting there saying
numbers to each other and then you’re laughing

00:15:17.880 --> 00:15:20.779
uproariously and I just don't get it.

00:15:20.779 --> 00:15:24.890
And they said, well we’ve known each other
so long that we’ve heard each other’s

00:15:24.890 --> 00:15:29.230
jokes at least a hundred times and so we have
them completely memorized, and so a couple

00:15:29.230 --> 00:15:33.290
of years ago we decided we’d just number
the damn things and it would take a lot less

00:15:33.290 --> 00:15:36.560
time to tell then and we could laugh more
that way.

00:15:36.560 --> 00:15:43.089
So, it’s an interesting little joke because
it sheds some light on the nature of deep

00:15:43.089 --> 00:15:49.210
relationships, and if you have a deep relationship
with someone, here’s how you can think about

00:15:49.210 --> 00:15:50.300
it.

00:15:50.300 --> 00:15:54.790
Imagine you're trying to analyze an essay,
and I’ve actually built an essay writing

00:15:54.790 --> 00:15:59.899
module which is on my site - 230 website.

00:15:59.899 --> 00:16:03.300
I don’t remember which template it is but
I’ll tell you in a while.

00:16:03.300 --> 00:16:08.360
It’s based on this theory of meaning, roughly
speaking, and you should use it to write your

00:16:08.360 --> 00:16:11.230
essay because if you do, it’ll be a way
better essay.

00:16:11.230 --> 00:16:12.449
Here’s the issue.

00:16:12.449 --> 00:16:16.360
If you're reading an essay, where is the meaning?

00:16:16.360 --> 00:16:22.060
Well, that’s a hard one because it might
be a hard one.

00:16:22.060 --> 00:16:26.230
So, the question might be badly formulated
in some sense because it might be many places.

00:16:26.230 --> 00:16:28.670
But, let’s think it through logically.

00:16:28.670 --> 00:16:32.860
Well you need the letters if it’s written
in alphabetic script.

00:16:32.860 --> 00:16:36.829
So obviously some of the meaning is in the
letters, and then the letters are combined

00:16:36.829 --> 00:16:40.600
in certain particular ways and that gives
you some words, and the words are combined

00:16:40.600 --> 00:16:44.220
in some particular ways and that gives you
phrases, and the phrases are joined together

00:16:44.220 --> 00:16:49.600
in sentences, and the sentences are strung
together in some reasonably coherent manner.

00:16:49.600 --> 00:16:53.310
So they kind of go from start to end in a
paragraph, and then the paragraphs are strung

00:16:53.310 --> 00:16:58.310
together in a coherent manner to make up the
totality of the essay.

00:16:58.310 --> 00:17:02.410
One of the things I noticed when I tried to
figure out how to grade essays and how to

00:17:02.410 --> 00:17:07.939
assess them is that you could do a good or
a bad job at any one of those levels.

00:17:07.939 --> 00:17:14.490
Like a really bad essay is full of spelling
mistakes, the words are inappropriate because

00:17:14.490 --> 00:17:17.939
the person doesn't actually understand them
so they're awkwardly used, the phrases are

00:17:17.939 --> 00:17:22.459
put together in a cliched manner, the sentence
structure isn't good.

00:17:22.459 --> 00:17:27.039
The sentences aren't even in the right paragraphs,
much less organized properly.

00:17:27.039 --> 00:17:32.480
The paragraphs don't flow from one to another
in what might be called - that’s kind of

00:17:32.480 --> 00:17:34.410
where the skeleton of the story is.

00:17:34.410 --> 00:17:39.000
It’s sort of the most important level in
some sense, and even if that wasn't all wrong

00:17:39.000 --> 00:17:42.000
there’s actually no idea there anyways.

00:17:42.000 --> 00:17:50.539
And then you can also read essays that have
one or more of those flaws or some combination

00:17:50.539 --> 00:17:51.539
of all of them.

00:17:51.539 --> 00:17:56.210
So very frequently, I’d read a student’s
essays and this often happens with smart,

00:17:56.210 --> 00:18:04.840
creative students who are very badly educated,
and that would be probably fifty percent of

00:18:04.840 --> 00:18:06.960
the people in this class, I would bet.

00:18:06.960 --> 00:18:11.200
I say that for genuine reasons because I run
a fourth year seminar where I have people

00:18:11.200 --> 00:18:17.220
write extensively, so what they do is they
write a four page essay, and then I complain

00:18:17.220 --> 00:18:21.480
about it at all those levels of analysis,
and then they write the same essay again in

00:18:21.480 --> 00:18:23.700
six pages, and then I do the same thing, and
then I do the same thing and then they do

00:18:23.700 --> 00:18:29.830
the same thing in eight pages, and half of
the first essays are terrible.

00:18:29.830 --> 00:18:31.720
That's partly because students do triage.

00:18:31.720 --> 00:18:34.340
They think, well do I really have to work
in this course?

00:18:34.340 --> 00:18:39.260
I’ll try a bit and if I get slapped, I’ll
work a little harder, and if I don’t, I’ll

00:18:39.260 --> 00:18:43.020
devote my time to other courses which is a
perfectly reasonable thing to do, but some

00:18:43.020 --> 00:18:44.920
of it is just pure inability.

00:18:44.920 --> 00:18:48.160
But what is really fascinating is by the end
of the course is that all of the students

00:18:48.160 --> 00:18:53.309
are writing well, and it’s pathetic as far
as I’m concerned because it meant that everybody

00:18:53.309 --> 00:18:57.490
could have been taught to write ten years
ago but they weren't.

00:18:57.490 --> 00:19:02.760
Anyways if you look at this template that
I laid out, then it will help you in this

00:19:02.760 --> 00:19:11.740
because it walks you through the process of
writing properly.

00:19:11.740 --> 00:19:14.590
So now and then I would read an essay and
everything is wrong about it, but there's

00:19:14.590 --> 00:19:19.620
a really smart about it, and knowing this
multilevel idea with regards to meaning, I

00:19:19.620 --> 00:19:23.200
can say, look you’re smarter than you can
write.

00:19:23.200 --> 00:19:27.140
There's really something here, but here's
all the things that are wrong with it.

00:19:27.140 --> 00:19:31.170
Then you get a worse situation in some sense
where everything about the essay is good but

00:19:31.170 --> 00:19:34.660
the idea isn’t, and so the words are good,
the phrases.

00:19:34.660 --> 00:19:38.170
The sentences are right, and it goes together
but it adds up to nothing.

00:19:38.170 --> 00:19:42.600
And those are hard to grade because you give
them a C maybe or a B, cause I’d probably

00:19:42.600 --> 00:19:46.370
give them a B, and the person comes up and
says, where did I make a mistake?

00:19:46.370 --> 00:19:52.270
It’s like, well you didn't really make a
mistake, you just didn't do anything right,

00:19:52.270 --> 00:19:55.480
and that's a whole different problem.

00:19:55.480 --> 00:19:57.980
So you think, that’s pretty complex with
regards to an essay.

00:19:57.980 --> 00:19:58.980
Where’s the meaning?

00:19:58.980 --> 00:20:01.650
But that isn't where the meaning is.

00:20:01.650 --> 00:20:04.170
Here, let me give you an example.

00:20:04.170 --> 00:20:05.530
I was thinking of this the other day.

00:20:05.530 --> 00:20:09.860
So, the ethnicity and the cultural backgrounds
of my students have changed quite radically

00:20:09.860 --> 00:20:11.080
over the years.

00:20:11.080 --> 00:20:15.230
When I started teaching this course for example
- and my personality course as well which

00:20:15.230 --> 00:20:19.220
would have been thirty years ago - I can take
it for granted that everyone in the room would

00:20:19.220 --> 00:20:22.919
know the basic biblical stories, and they
would know the Judeo-Christian version of

00:20:22.919 --> 00:20:25.180
them and not, say the Islamic version of them.

00:20:25.180 --> 00:20:31.100
Well that's gone, and that's really made it
difficult for me in some ways because there's

00:20:31.100 --> 00:20:32.100
a bunch of things.

00:20:32.100 --> 00:20:37.280
There’s a bunch of collective knowledge
that might constitute a shared set of cultural

00:20:37.280 --> 00:20:41.111
assumptions that I can no longer presume and
it means there's all sorts of things that

00:20:41.111 --> 00:20:48.300
I can't talk about or if I’m going to talk
about, I have to step way back and start from

00:20:48.300 --> 00:20:50.090
first principles let’s say.

00:20:50.090 --> 00:20:55.010
So that’s also made me think because if
I - let’s say you write me an essay and

00:20:55.010 --> 00:20:56.990
let’s say it’s a Psychology essay.

00:20:56.990 --> 00:21:00.059
Now you're gonna presume that there's a whole
bunch of things I know about Psychology that

00:21:00.059 --> 00:21:01.970
you also know.

00:21:01.970 --> 00:21:05.740
And the reason you're going to presume that
is because, well we come - for the purpose

00:21:05.740 --> 00:21:11.610
of this conversation - we come from the same
cultural background and what that means is

00:21:11.610 --> 00:21:17.539
we share a set of assumptions, and we don't
have to say what those assumptions are.

00:21:17.539 --> 00:21:21.350
We can assume them and then we can talk about
the things that we disagree about, and of

00:21:21.350 --> 00:21:24.289
course that's what you're doing when you're
writing an essay or having a conversation

00:21:24.289 --> 00:21:25.289
right?

00:21:25.289 --> 00:21:29.400
You're saying well obviously we don't have
to talk about what we know because we already

00:21:29.400 --> 00:21:30.400
know it.

00:21:30.400 --> 00:21:34.630
We want to push that a little bit so we start
to talk a little bit about the things we don’t

00:21:34.630 --> 00:21:40.700
know, but no more so than we both understand.

00:21:40.700 --> 00:21:44.050
So then you think damn it, the meaning of
the essay isn’t in the essay at all.

00:21:44.050 --> 00:21:48.460
It’s in the interaction between the shared
cultural assumptions between the writer and

00:21:48.460 --> 00:21:50.950
the reader and the essay.

00:21:50.950 --> 00:21:54.630
Well and then you think, it’s worse than
that even because the shared cultural assumptions

00:21:54.630 --> 00:22:02.710
are in their own sort of network, and then
that's nested inside a very an extraordinarily

00:22:02.710 --> 00:22:09.400
broad biological network and it’s like actually,
you can continue expanding the outer reaches

00:22:09.400 --> 00:22:13.630
of the essay without limit.

00:22:13.630 --> 00:22:18.900
So part of that means it’s actually impossible
to specify the meaning of the essay.

00:22:18.900 --> 00:22:25.250
It’s something you both work at in combination,
as writer and as reader, but it’s very fluid

00:22:25.250 --> 00:22:26.710
and flexible.

00:22:26.710 --> 00:22:31.510
That problem is partly why the post modernists
got going because they sort of noticed this.

00:22:31.510 --> 00:22:37.409
Corrupt as they are, they did notice at one
point that it was very very difficult to say

00:22:37.409 --> 00:22:45.520
what any given phrase, paragraph, or essay
mean because it wasn't there - like it’s

00:22:45.520 --> 00:22:46.660
not in the book.

00:22:46.660 --> 00:22:53.500
It’s sort of in the book, but - you know
this too if you listen to a piece of music.

00:22:53.500 --> 00:22:58.440
So let’s say you had a favorite song when
you were fourteen, and when you first heard

00:22:58.440 --> 00:23:02.240
that song, maybe you didn’t like it that
much, but you maybe heard it four times and

00:23:02.240 --> 00:23:04.010
thought man that’s one excellent song.

00:23:04.010 --> 00:23:08.340
Then at some point, you've heard it so many
times that you never want to hear it again.

00:23:08.340 --> 00:23:12.559
That’s interesting too because it means
the song has transformed in relationship to

00:23:12.559 --> 00:23:19.200
your historical interactions with it and you
might say, no this song is the same.

00:23:19.200 --> 00:23:22.510
Well, it depends on what you mean by the song.

00:23:22.510 --> 00:23:24.510
The notes are the same.

00:23:24.510 --> 00:23:27.530
The experience has certainly transformed.

00:23:27.530 --> 00:23:31.210
So anyways, back to the couple that are sitting
there talking.

00:23:31.210 --> 00:23:33.789
They're having this argument and they're trying
to sort out what this is about.

00:23:33.789 --> 00:23:38.169
Well the problem is it’s about everything
the argument is about plus everything that’s

00:23:38.169 --> 00:23:42.100
ever happened to them in their relationship.

00:23:42.100 --> 00:23:48.610
If someone has told you the same thing fifty
times, it is not the same thing as the thing

00:23:48.610 --> 00:23:50.480
they told you the first time.

00:23:50.480 --> 00:23:55.310
God only knows how many transformations it’s
gone through as it’s been continually repeated,

00:23:55.310 --> 00:23:59.669
and how many different arguments it’s been
embedded in, and how many things you’d have

00:23:59.669 --> 00:24:03.029
to know to understand the shades of intonation
in all of that.

00:24:03.029 --> 00:24:10.570
But basically each utterance a couple produces
is - it’s meaning is determined by the entire

00:24:10.570 --> 00:24:14.140
history of interactions that those two people
have had.

00:24:14.140 --> 00:24:16.900
So, how are you going to specify that?

00:24:16.900 --> 00:24:19.799
It’s very very difficult to specify it.

00:24:19.799 --> 00:24:25.320
Now, you really can’t, and it’s also difficult
to know when you’ve specified it enough.

00:24:25.320 --> 00:24:33.090
You know, one of the things that is interesting
training as a psychoanalyst is that it started

00:24:33.090 --> 00:24:40.480
to become absolutely mind boggling how much
archaeology you can undertake underneath a

00:24:40.480 --> 00:24:45.730
single statement, especially if it’s delivered
with a bit of edge and some contempt.

00:24:45.730 --> 00:24:51.860
It’s like there’s just levels and levels
and levels and levels of meaning underneath

00:24:51.860 --> 00:24:55.640
that, and you can tell that when you’re
engaged in an argument like that because someone

00:24:55.640 --> 00:25:01.930
will trigger you, let’s say, for lack of
a better word, and they’ll have broken a

00:25:01.930 --> 00:25:05.460
rule that you have established with them and
you’re both in a pit.

00:25:05.460 --> 00:25:10.500
The fight is about that pit, even though maybe
it’s about some surface thing, and you're

00:25:10.500 --> 00:25:13.780
just running around there like rats that are
lit on fire and there’s nothing you can

00:25:13.780 --> 00:25:14.850
do about it.

00:25:14.850 --> 00:25:20.750
And it’s a consequence of all the issues
that are lurking beneath the surface of that

00:25:20.750 --> 00:25:24.360
speech act that seemed to be something - you
could capture it on a video.

00:25:24.360 --> 00:25:26.360
You can say, well here’s what you said.

00:25:26.360 --> 00:25:28.000
There's a video tape recording of it.

00:25:28.000 --> 00:25:32.820
It’s like no, that's not what you said because
the videotape cannot capture the context,

00:25:32.820 --> 00:25:33.820
and it can’t.

00:25:33.820 --> 00:25:38.370
So you can't even get an objective record
of the meaning of the conversation because

00:25:38.370 --> 00:25:41.440
the meaning isn’t right in the conversation.

00:25:41.440 --> 00:25:43.700
Okay so that’s one of the problems.

00:25:43.700 --> 00:25:47.960
One of the problems we’re trying to validate
moral claims is that you’ve got this insane

00:25:47.960 --> 00:25:53.810
contextual complexity that’s almost impossible
to dispose of, and so you can say - you can

00:25:53.810 --> 00:25:59.169
even say that if person A did action A in
situation A, that’d be right and then person

00:25:59.169 --> 00:26:02.900
B did action A in situation A, that would
be wrong.

00:26:02.900 --> 00:26:06.630
It would depend on the person, and so it’s
very easy to slip from that into a kind of

00:26:06.630 --> 00:26:11.590
moral relativism because how in the world
do you deal with the complexity and how do

00:26:11.590 --> 00:26:13.340
you make a truth claim?

00:26:13.340 --> 00:26:18.970
So I would really say in some sense that’s
the intellectual situation in modern universities.

00:26:18.970 --> 00:26:23.200
Now, the other thing that’s kind of interesting
is that this was actually discovered in a

00:26:23.200 --> 00:26:29.470
number of modern fields simultaneously, so
just as the literary theorists started to

00:26:29.470 --> 00:26:34.950
discover, say after World War II, that it
wasn’t enough to say that a book had meaning

00:26:34.950 --> 00:26:35.950
in it.

00:26:35.950 --> 00:26:38.950
Like there was something wrong with that,
and there isn't even a single meaning, there’s

00:26:38.950 --> 00:26:43.450
a multiplicity of meanings and it isn't even
a stable multiplicity of meanings, people

00:26:43.450 --> 00:26:46.700
were finding that out in other domains as
well.

00:26:46.700 --> 00:26:53.289
For example in artificial intelligence where
it became very obvious, very rapidly that

00:26:53.289 --> 00:26:58.309
perceiving a simple object was not simple
because objects aren’t simple, and the border

00:26:58.309 --> 00:27:01.299
between one object and another is in some
sense arbitrary.

00:27:01.299 --> 00:27:06.591
I mean, when I look at each of you, I sort
of see you as entities, but that’s because

00:27:06.591 --> 00:27:10.450
I see you at this level of resolution.

00:27:10.450 --> 00:27:13.539
You're cellular masses at a different level
of resolution.

00:27:13.539 --> 00:27:17.419
You're molecular composites at a different
level of resolution.

00:27:17.419 --> 00:27:24.510
You’re made up of ten times as many bacteria
as cells at another level of resolution, so

00:27:24.510 --> 00:27:27.690
really you’re more like an ocean than you
are like an entity.

00:27:27.690 --> 00:27:31.420
And then, well is it you or your family that’s
the real.

00:27:31.420 --> 00:27:33.570
Or is it you, your family, and your culture?

00:27:33.570 --> 00:27:38.940
What about your species, or are you a mammal.

00:27:38.940 --> 00:27:45.640
It’s like all of those things and any of
those things at the same time, so when I look

00:27:45.640 --> 00:27:49.140
at you, what am I supposed to see?

00:27:49.140 --> 00:27:58.580
Do I see individual with name or do I see
ethnicity or do I see - well you get the picture.

00:27:58.580 --> 00:28:03.120
So object perception turns out to be extraordinarily
difficult because things aren't just simple

00:28:03.120 --> 00:28:07.530
objects even if they’re relatively simple.

00:28:07.530 --> 00:28:11.010
So, you throw your hands.

00:28:11.010 --> 00:28:13.299
You think how can there be any harmony.

00:28:13.299 --> 00:28:15.770
How can you draw some sort of sense from that?

00:28:15.770 --> 00:28:19.500
Okay, so that’s the argument against drawing
any sense out of it.

00:28:19.500 --> 00:28:21.700
But then, let’s try the other attack.

00:28:21.700 --> 00:28:28.410
Let’s see if we can make a case for there
being some claims of - that moral claims can

00:28:28.410 --> 00:28:29.410
be validated.

00:28:29.410 --> 00:28:32.659
Okay, so here’s a question for you.

00:28:32.659 --> 00:28:36.070
If you took a bunch of chimpanzees that didn't
know each other and you threw them in this

00:28:36.070 --> 00:28:39.090
classroom, what you would have is a war.

00:28:39.090 --> 00:28:40.090
Right.

00:28:40.090 --> 00:28:45.710
Now, you're roughly in that situation, not
exactly because A - you’re not chimpanzees,

00:28:45.710 --> 00:28:52.370
but you know your genetic material is quite
reasonably related.

00:28:52.370 --> 00:28:56.390
You only split off your common ancestors seven
million years ago, and that seems like a long

00:28:56.390 --> 00:28:59.070
time and it is, but it’s not three hundred
million years ago.

00:28:59.070 --> 00:29:03.299
It’s still relatively recently from the
evolutionary perspective.

00:29:03.299 --> 00:29:06.049
Here you all are, you don’t really know
each other.

00:29:06.049 --> 00:29:09.340
You obviously share important elements of
identity.

00:29:09.340 --> 00:29:13.110
So you’re all students, and you can come
in here and all of a sudden you all know what

00:29:13.110 --> 00:29:15.510
to do, and no one fights.

00:29:15.510 --> 00:29:19.659
Or if they do fight, it’s - there are very
constrained rules around the fighting and

00:29:19.659 --> 00:29:25.669
everyone accepts them, and it seems hard to
say that’s not better than the alternative.

00:29:25.669 --> 00:29:33.820
So unless you wanna come in here and have
an absolute chaotic war.

00:29:33.820 --> 00:29:38.160
Somehow we’ve managed to figure out how
we can figure out how to bring relative strangers

00:29:38.160 --> 00:29:42.880
together in a relatively high pressure situation
because we’re learning about complicated

00:29:42.880 --> 00:29:47.480
and challenging things, and everybody gets
along.

00:29:47.480 --> 00:29:48.990
And everybody’s kind of happy about that.

00:29:48.990 --> 00:29:54.230
So it looks like we can figure out patterns
of behavior that, well, then the question

00:29:54.230 --> 00:29:55.530
is, are they correct?

00:29:55.530 --> 00:29:57.000
Are they morally correct?

00:29:57.000 --> 00:29:59.260
Are they right, or did they work?

00:29:59.260 --> 00:30:03.400
And you might also ask yourself what’s the
difference between all of those claims, is

00:30:03.400 --> 00:30:06.600
there similarity or difference between those
claims.

00:30:06.600 --> 00:30:10.860
So, and then you might ask yourself - okay
so here’s something else you might think

00:30:10.860 --> 00:30:12.630
about.

00:30:12.630 --> 00:30:17.830
So, let’s say you’re playing Monopoly.

00:30:17.830 --> 00:30:20.120
How many of you have played Monopoly in your
life?

00:30:20.120 --> 00:30:22.940
Okay, is there anyone here who hasn't played
Monopoly?

00:30:22.940 --> 00:30:23.940
Excellent.

00:30:23.940 --> 00:30:27.290
A cultural universal.

00:30:27.290 --> 00:30:36.510
So, obviously in some ways it doesn't matter
if you get all those little hotels right?

00:30:36.510 --> 00:30:42.080
But, on the other hand if you act like it
doesn't matter while you're playing Monopoly,

00:30:42.080 --> 00:30:45.730
then people hit you with things and they should
because part of the rule is that you're gonna

00:30:45.730 --> 00:30:49.340
act like this matters because if you don't,
then it’s not any fun.

00:30:49.340 --> 00:30:54.630
So you have to act like it matters and then
it turns out when you start playing, it matters.

00:30:54.630 --> 00:30:58.220
People will actually - they’ll get angry,
especially if you cheat.

00:30:58.220 --> 00:30:59.529
But people will play Monopoly.

00:30:59.529 --> 00:31:02.049
They’ll act as if the endpoint matters.

00:31:02.049 --> 00:31:04.909
You might say, well what is the endpoint?

00:31:04.909 --> 00:31:11.470
And one answer is to get all the hotels and
all the money, so you enter the 1%, so you

00:31:11.470 --> 00:31:15.480
have all the wealth and no one has any, and
so you win and you feel happy about that,

00:31:15.480 --> 00:31:19.080
even if you're a radical left winger.

00:31:19.080 --> 00:31:22.029
So that's kind of interesting.

00:31:22.029 --> 00:31:23.580
Then you say, well that's not really the point.

00:31:23.580 --> 00:31:29.220
The point is to enjoy the game, and you say
yeah okay that’s probably the point too.

00:31:29.220 --> 00:31:31.700
And you might say, well that's not really
the point either.

00:31:31.700 --> 00:31:35.890
You want to hone your negotiating skills.

00:31:35.890 --> 00:31:38.410
You want to spend some time with your friends.

00:31:38.410 --> 00:31:42.700
There's lots of things going on at once, but
it doesn't really matter because Monopoly

00:31:42.700 --> 00:31:44.529
is a game that you’ll play.

00:31:44.529 --> 00:31:52.770
Now imagine instead that Monopoly - here's
the new rule for Monopoly.

00:31:52.770 --> 00:31:58.360
All the people you're playing with can randomly
steal your hotels and houses and your money

00:31:58.360 --> 00:32:02.500
whenever they want, and you can steal it back.

00:32:02.500 --> 00:32:11.640
So then you think, well no one’s gonna play
that game, and then you think well why not?

00:32:11.640 --> 00:32:17.750
Well, the first surface answer is that’s
a stupid game, and you know that somehow.

00:32:17.750 --> 00:32:21.980
You think that’s a stupid game, but then
you start to think about well why is that

00:32:21.980 --> 00:32:22.980
a stupid game?

00:32:22.980 --> 00:32:27.510
Or you might say it’s an unplayable game
which would be a little bit more sophisticated.

00:32:27.510 --> 00:32:29.320
Why would that not work?

00:32:29.320 --> 00:32:33.000
And you might say, well what’s the point
in getting all the hotels if someone could

00:32:33.000 --> 00:32:34.760
just take them?

00:32:34.760 --> 00:32:37.340
But you might say what’s the point in getting
the hotels anyway?

00:32:37.340 --> 00:32:38.769
Like who cares about the damn hotels.

00:32:38.769 --> 00:32:40.390
So it’s a bit tricky.

00:32:40.390 --> 00:32:45.460
There’s something about the randomness of
what’s now allowed and the unpredictability

00:32:45.460 --> 00:32:50.670
of it and the arbitrary nature of it and its
lack of a relationship with your own actions

00:32:50.670 --> 00:32:53.700
that takes all the motivational impetus out
of the game.

00:32:53.700 --> 00:32:59.279
So one thing we might think about to begin
with, is there’s a difference between a

00:32:59.279 --> 00:33:05.870
good game and a bad game, and there's also
a difference between a game and no game.

00:33:05.870 --> 00:33:10.860
And we don’t know what the difference is
exactly, but there is a difference.

00:33:10.860 --> 00:33:15.340
We might even say not only are there good
games and bad games, some good games are better

00:33:15.340 --> 00:33:16.659
than other games.

00:33:16.659 --> 00:33:19.860
How many of you have played Settlers of Catan?

00:33:19.860 --> 00:33:23.309
Okay, is that better than Monopoly or worse?

00:33:23.309 --> 00:33:24.649
Student says: It’s better.

00:33:24.649 --> 00:33:25.649
better.

00:33:25.649 --> 00:33:27.590
Does anyone think it’s not better?

00:33:27.590 --> 00:33:30.950
Okay, why is it better?

00:33:30.950 --> 00:33:33.960
What’s better about it?

00:33:33.960 --> 00:33:45.279
It’s more complex and the part where you
said - I mean it’s not necessarily stealing

00:33:45.279 --> 00:33:47.840
but there’s a piece of the game in there,
I think it’s like the robber or something

00:33:47.840 --> 00:33:48.840
like that.

00:33:48.840 --> 00:33:49.840
Yeah, there’s a robber.
Student says: And you place him on one of

00:33:49.840 --> 00:33:50.840
the tiles.

00:33:50.840 --> 00:33:52.919
You can mess with people, so that’s fun.

00:33:52.919 --> 00:33:54.990
It is fun.

00:33:54.990 --> 00:33:57.590
It’s like I’m gonna get you with a robber.

00:33:57.590 --> 00:34:03.620
It’s more akin to real like I think, like
in Monopoly

00:34:03.620 --> 00:34:05.510
No, that’s reasonable.

00:34:05.510 --> 00:34:07.590
It’s also got a strategic element.

00:34:07.590 --> 00:34:11.589
How many of you play Risk?

00:34:11.589 --> 00:34:17.139
Okay, Settlers of Catan or Risk?

00:34:17.139 --> 00:34:19.179
Okay, two Risk diehards.

00:34:19.179 --> 00:34:20.659
What about the rest of you?

00:34:20.659 --> 00:34:22.339
How many are for Risk?

00:34:22.339 --> 00:34:23.520
Two.

00:34:23.520 --> 00:34:25.099
How many for Settlers of Catan.

00:34:25.099 --> 00:34:26.560
Okay, okay.

00:34:26.560 --> 00:34:31.220
Well, Settlers of Catan is a bit like Risk
because it has a strategic element and there's

00:34:31.220 --> 00:34:35.850
a territorial expansion that goes along with
it, and there’s also trading which is one

00:34:35.850 --> 00:34:38.730
of the things that's nice about Monopoly and
about Risk.

00:34:38.730 --> 00:34:43.850
Okay but well, you can see - you have intuitions
about what constitutes a better game and part

00:34:43.850 --> 00:34:48.490
of the way you develop those intuitions is
not exactly philosophical because I doubt

00:34:48.490 --> 00:34:52.679
if you really thought through why Settlers
of Catan is a better game than, say, Risk

00:34:52.679 --> 00:34:58.390
or Monopoly for those of that think it does,
but you watch your own reaction and your own

00:34:58.390 --> 00:35:02.690
reaction is okay well I’d rather play Settlers
of Catan.

00:35:02.690 --> 00:35:05.440
You might ask yourself why, but you don’t
have to.

00:35:05.440 --> 00:35:08.390
You’d just rather play it.

00:35:08.390 --> 00:35:11.470
So then you think well what does it mean that
you’d rather play it?

00:35:11.470 --> 00:35:14.460
Well it’s more fun, it’s more enjoyable.

00:35:14.460 --> 00:35:18.510
If asked voluntarily, that's the one I would
pick, but you really don't know why, apart

00:35:18.510 --> 00:35:25.970
from those reasons but obviously those are
complex - that’s a complex social engagement,

00:35:25.970 --> 00:35:26.970
those games.

00:35:26.970 --> 00:35:27.970
They’re complex.

00:35:27.970 --> 00:35:34.651
In fact they’re so complex, you can have
preferences and not even know why, and that’s

00:35:34.651 --> 00:35:35.651
interesting.

00:35:35.651 --> 00:35:41.420
I think the idea that there are games, good
games and bad games and games and non-games,

00:35:41.420 --> 00:35:46.160
is a nice way of starting to conceptualize
what the difference might be between moral

00:35:46.160 --> 00:35:49.720
systems because a game is actually a moral
system.

00:35:49.720 --> 00:35:51.150
It has a point.

00:35:51.150 --> 00:35:52.900
It has an end.

00:35:52.900 --> 00:35:58.600
You have to cooperate and compete to run through
the game.

00:35:58.600 --> 00:36:07.180
So awhile back, a psychologist actually started
this and it’s reprehensible, but psychologists

00:36:07.180 --> 00:36:11.130
have done lots of reprehensible things and
this is one of them.

00:36:11.130 --> 00:36:14.600
This psychologist, whose name I don't remember
at the moment, was reviewing the literature

00:36:14.600 --> 00:36:20.570
on the difference in aggression between boys
and girls and men and women.

00:36:20.570 --> 00:36:25.720
Boys and men are much more aggressive, depending
on how you define as aggressive, than girls

00:36:25.720 --> 00:36:26.950
and women.

00:36:26.950 --> 00:36:31.270
Now that tends to be mostly true with physical
aggression.

00:36:31.270 --> 00:36:35.369
So you might say kicking, hitting, biting,
punching, stealing.

00:36:35.369 --> 00:36:40.330
Two year old boys for example - two year olds,
the ones who do most of that are boys.

00:36:40.330 --> 00:36:44.920
Now, the two year old girls are cutting each
other up behind their backs but no one measures

00:36:44.920 --> 00:36:45.920
that.

00:36:45.920 --> 00:36:50.680
So you find for example, with female aggression
it tends to be relationship savaging, and

00:36:50.680 --> 00:36:52.780
you see that in the playground.

00:36:52.780 --> 00:36:53.780
You see it in junior high.

00:36:53.780 --> 00:36:55.510
You see it in high school, and you see it
on the net.

00:36:55.510 --> 00:36:57.380
It’s always the same thing.

00:36:57.380 --> 00:37:01.780
Whether men are more aggressive than women,
it depends on how you define aggression, but

00:37:01.780 --> 00:37:04.000
men are more physically aggressive anyways.

00:37:04.000 --> 00:37:12.740
Awhile back, there was this idea that maybe
we could dampen down the male aggression by

00:37:12.740 --> 00:37:18.660
socializing little boys more like little girls,
and one of the offshoots of that was that

00:37:18.660 --> 00:37:22.119
we should only let children play cooperative
games.

00:37:22.119 --> 00:37:26.470
Now, the first thing you might notice is that
anything you might have done as a game was

00:37:26.470 --> 00:37:28.250
not cooperative.

00:37:28.250 --> 00:37:33.470
Right, like it’s not a game if it’s just
cooperative, but then the other thing you

00:37:33.470 --> 00:37:38.670
might notice is that the distinction between
a cooperative and a competitive game is far

00:37:38.670 --> 00:37:42.680
more sophisticated than people that come up
with those dimwitted theories of how to improve

00:37:42.680 --> 00:37:44.700
the world would ever appreciate.

00:37:44.700 --> 00:37:47.050
So here’s an example.

00:37:47.050 --> 00:37:49.320
Is hockey a game?

00:37:49.320 --> 00:37:50.320
Yes.

00:37:50.320 --> 00:37:51.320
Is it an aggressive game?

00:37:51.320 --> 00:37:52.320
Yes.

00:37:52.320 --> 00:37:53.320
Is it a competitive game?

00:37:53.320 --> 00:37:54.320
Yes.

00:37:54.320 --> 00:37:55.320
Is it a cooperative game?

00:37:55.320 --> 00:37:56.320
Yes.

00:37:56.320 --> 00:37:57.320
Aha.

00:37:57.320 --> 00:37:58.320
Why?

00:37:58.320 --> 00:37:59.320
Why is it a cooperative game?

00:37:59.320 --> 00:38:01.220
Because you have to work together.

00:38:01.220 --> 00:38:03.230
Right, so there's the team right.

00:38:03.230 --> 00:38:07.260
You’re also obviously cooperating with your
team members, but you're also maybe trying

00:38:07.260 --> 00:38:11.400
to be the best player, but you're also trying
to be the best player in a way that doesn't

00:38:11.400 --> 00:38:16.320
irritate the hell out of your team players
right, because there's something about that

00:38:16.320 --> 00:38:17.500
that’s counter productive.

00:38:17.500 --> 00:38:23.211
Like, even if you're a good player and you’re
a prima donna and you hog the puck, say, and

00:38:23.211 --> 00:38:28.030
maybe you're not interested in the development
of your other team members, like you’re

00:38:28.030 --> 00:38:30.300
not a good player even if you are the most
talented.

00:38:30.300 --> 00:38:32.410
So, there’s obviously a cooperative element.

00:38:32.410 --> 00:38:34.170
Is there a cooperative element beyond that?

00:38:34.170 --> 00:38:39.970
Well yeah, you have to all agree to sort of
cooperate in so much as agreeing to the rules

00:38:39.970 --> 00:38:42.300
of the game and playing it.

00:38:42.300 --> 00:38:43.300
Right.

00:38:43.300 --> 00:38:45.620
You don't bring a chessboard or a basketball.

00:38:45.620 --> 00:38:46.620
Right.

00:38:46.620 --> 00:38:51.330
No matter if you're on different teams because
you're all there to play hockey.

00:38:51.330 --> 00:38:55.910
So just to play hockey is a cooperative endeavor
because think of all the other things you

00:38:55.910 --> 00:38:58.180
could be doing instead of playing hockey at
that time.

00:38:58.180 --> 00:39:01.720
You could be doing an infinite number of other
things so really if you look at the relationship

00:39:01.720 --> 00:39:08.579
between cooperation and competition in any
collaborative human affair, things are usually

00:39:08.579 --> 00:39:12.480
competitive at one level, cooperative at another,
competitive at another, like there’s this

00:39:12.480 --> 00:39:17.640
continual interplay between competition and
cooperation and you can't disentangle them.

00:39:17.640 --> 00:39:22.040
Now one of the things that Piaget said - Piaget
is a, for those of you who don’t know and

00:39:22.040 --> 00:39:26.310
you probably all do know, is perhaps the foremost
developmental psychologist of the 20th century

00:39:26.310 --> 00:39:29.630
even though he didn't think he was a psychologist.

00:39:29.630 --> 00:39:34.950
Piaget actually made the claim, the radical
claim, that two year olds for example - this

00:39:34.950 --> 00:39:38.490
isn’t Piaget’s claim but it’s associated.

00:39:38.490 --> 00:39:41.440
If you put people together, and their age
matched.

00:39:41.440 --> 00:39:46.790
So you got a group of nine months olds, say,
one year olds, two year olds, three year olds,

00:39:46.790 --> 00:39:50.680
all the way up to eighteen, and you just throw
them in a room, maybe with some things of

00:39:50.680 --> 00:39:56.790
them to do and you count the number of violent
acts, which is kicking, hitting, biting, or

00:39:56.790 --> 00:39:57.790
stealing.

00:39:57.790 --> 00:40:03.800
Okay, two year olds, they’re the most violent
human beings and most of them aren't actually

00:40:03.800 --> 00:40:05.220
violent.

00:40:05.220 --> 00:40:06.670
There's a subset of them that are violent.

00:40:06.670 --> 00:40:10.600
It’s about five to ten percent of them,
and almost all of them are boys, and then

00:40:10.600 --> 00:40:16.430
if you track those boys across time, over
the next two years most of them become civilized

00:40:16.430 --> 00:40:20.200
and the ones that don't never become civilized.

00:40:20.200 --> 00:40:24.130
So they're kind of antisocial throughout their
childhood, and then when testosterone kicks

00:40:24.130 --> 00:40:29.579
in at about fourteen and all the boys get
antisocial again, they catch up to the always

00:40:29.579 --> 00:40:34.710
antisocial boys and the normal boys taper
off quite dramatically at around nineteen

00:40:34.710 --> 00:40:38.550
and flatten right out at about twenty seven
but the antisocial boys, they just keep being

00:40:38.550 --> 00:40:39.840
antisocial.

00:40:39.840 --> 00:40:51.499
So now, Piaget noted that there was a dramatic
transformation in behavior - individual behavior

00:40:51.499 --> 00:40:56.160
- in relationship to the social world between
the ages of two and four.

00:40:56.160 --> 00:41:01.720
And, if you watch two year olds play together,
what they did fundamentally is like a parallel

00:41:01.720 --> 00:41:02.790
play.

00:41:02.790 --> 00:41:07.220
So maybe, there's one two year old there and
another two year old there and that two year

00:41:07.220 --> 00:41:11.079
old is playing truck and this two year old
is playing ambulance or something or maybe

00:41:11.079 --> 00:41:15.410
playing doll, and they're sitting with each
other, and maybe they interact a little bit

00:41:15.410 --> 00:41:18.569
but they're not playing the same game.

00:41:18.569 --> 00:41:25.430
Okay so, what they're doing in some sense
is they're engaged in a goal directed activity,

00:41:25.430 --> 00:41:28.450
and it has a point.

00:41:28.450 --> 00:41:34.640
They're sort of integrating all their interior
sub personalities in a sense that allows them

00:41:34.640 --> 00:41:38.030
to play a single game with themselves.

00:41:38.030 --> 00:41:44.231
That's pretty complex from a cognitive perspective
because they're not fractionated, and they're

00:41:44.231 --> 00:41:50.550
not being pulled out by hunger or anger or
sadness or fear or any of those things, and

00:41:50.550 --> 00:41:55.520
they're doing something that's highly complex
cognitively and they can organize themselves

00:41:55.520 --> 00:41:58.290
well enough to do it for a substantial period
of time.

00:41:58.290 --> 00:41:59.960
So it’s like it’s a major developmental
advance.

00:41:59.960 --> 00:42:02.860
But they can't do it with another person.

00:42:02.860 --> 00:42:09.300
Now one of Piaget’s hypotheses was that
they couldn't do it - they couldn't play the

00:42:09.300 --> 00:42:16.619
same game with another person without actually
setting up a competitive structure so actually

00:42:16.619 --> 00:42:19.839
competition was a prerequisite to competition.

00:42:19.839 --> 00:42:25.310
The idea was okay so, if you watch how kids
do this, you can get a sense of it.

00:42:25.310 --> 00:42:30.720
I mean, on the one hand they all get together
and they decide how to play tag.

00:42:30.720 --> 00:42:35.760
Everyone knows the rules for tag, and you
can win at tag, but without the point - the

00:42:35.760 --> 00:42:40.210
point in some sense is the construction of
a dominance hierarchy of competence.

00:42:40.210 --> 00:42:43.119
Someone wins and then there are sort of the
people who are second best and third best,

00:42:43.119 --> 00:42:45.290
and everybody's really into doing that.

00:42:45.290 --> 00:42:47.653
You all have to get together if you're kids
and you say well what should we do.

00:42:47.653 --> 00:42:51.490
You decide, well, we’re gonna play tag and
then you set up the rules because there’s

00:42:51.490 --> 00:42:55.210
infinite variations of tag, and everyone goes
off and play.

00:42:55.210 --> 00:42:59.590
So it’s a competitive game because only
one person can win, or maybe one at a time

00:42:59.590 --> 00:43:00.960
can win.

00:43:00.960 --> 00:43:04.820
But once you’ve all agreed on that, then
you’re cooperating.

00:43:04.820 --> 00:43:09.590
So that’s quite interesting, and Piaget
regarded that as a necessary stage of the

00:43:09.590 --> 00:43:12.290
ability to act like a social creature.

00:43:12.290 --> 00:43:16.579
Here’s something that happens if you’re
not a very popular kid.

00:43:16.579 --> 00:43:20.780
Let’s say four kids are playing Jacks.

00:43:20.780 --> 00:43:21.880
Good enough.

00:43:21.880 --> 00:43:24.150
I don't think kids play Jacks anymore but
whatever.

00:43:24.150 --> 00:43:28.099
If they were playing Jacks, they’re sittings
there playing Jacks, and when you play Jacks,

00:43:28.099 --> 00:43:32.010
you bounce a ball and you pick up a jack,
and if you manage that and a ball - if you

00:43:32.010 --> 00:43:35.200
manage that, you drop the ball and pick up
two jacks and drop the ball, and you keep

00:43:35.200 --> 00:43:38.599
going up till like eight jacks or whatever.

00:43:38.599 --> 00:43:43.490
So now another kid wants to play Jacks, and
we’ll say this is a relatively popular kid

00:43:43.490 --> 00:43:47.560
and so the kid sidles up to the group that’s
playing and sits down because they're all

00:43:47.560 --> 00:43:53.050
sitting down and then watches, and then maybe
watches jack-playing motions or a couple of

00:43:53.050 --> 00:43:58.650
comments about how the Jacks game is going,
and maybe the other kids don't have to disrupt

00:43:58.650 --> 00:44:02.150
what they're doing very much and they can
kind of open the circle, and let that kid

00:44:02.150 --> 00:44:05.599
scoot in and he gets to, or she gets to play
Jacks too.

00:44:05.599 --> 00:44:09.520
Whereas a kid that doesn't - and that often
doesn't happen, even if it’s a popular kid.

00:44:09.520 --> 00:44:12.550
Sometimes the little group that's already
formed won’t open and let them in.

00:44:12.550 --> 00:44:14.320
You've been to cocktail parties.

00:44:14.320 --> 00:44:15.650
It’s exactly the same thing.

00:44:15.650 --> 00:44:17.300
It’s exactly the same thing.

00:44:17.300 --> 00:44:21.109
So you’re supposed to hang around the edge
of the conversation and maybe if you've got

00:44:21.109 --> 00:44:26.230
something that isn't too stupid to say, you
can offer it and maybe they’d let you in.

00:44:26.230 --> 00:44:31.390
Okay, now if you're maybe not very social,
what you do is stand out there and you don't

00:44:31.390 --> 00:44:35.800
notice what the hell is going on and you don't
start to match your body to what’s happening,

00:44:35.800 --> 00:44:39.430
and maybe you say, I don't want to play Jacks,
that's a stupid game.

00:44:39.430 --> 00:44:43.351
Let's go do something else, in which case
the kids are not happy with you, and you're

00:44:43.351 --> 00:44:44.630
unpopular.

00:44:44.630 --> 00:44:45.630
And that’s that.

00:44:45.630 --> 00:44:49.780
You're stuck with it because if you've got
that by the time you’re four, the other

00:44:49.780 --> 00:44:53.240
kids are gonna leave you so far in the dust
you’re never going to catch up.

00:44:53.240 --> 00:44:56.329
Okay, so what have we concluded from that?

00:44:56.329 --> 00:45:00.260
We might conclude that people play games.

00:45:00.260 --> 00:45:01.810
There's lots of different games.

00:45:01.810 --> 00:45:04.380
There’s lots of different playable games.

00:45:04.380 --> 00:45:07.810
There might even be an infinite number of
playable games.

00:45:07.810 --> 00:45:11.400
There's a lot of non playable games, those
are the ones that aren’t any fun, and there's

00:45:11.400 --> 00:45:13.780
a lot of interactions that aren’t games.

00:45:13.780 --> 00:45:19.339
Okay so we can all accept that, and then we’ve
added to that the observation, or the presupposition,

00:45:19.339 --> 00:45:26.599
the ability to play games is a prerequisite
for being socialized, so being able to operate

00:45:26.599 --> 00:45:30.790
together with people and that that operation
has elements of competition and elements of

00:45:30.790 --> 00:45:32.290
cooperation.

00:45:32.290 --> 00:45:33.800
Reasonable?

00:45:33.800 --> 00:45:39.320
And then you might say, well everything people
do communally has a game like aspect, and

00:45:39.320 --> 00:45:43.530
you might say well that's actually why kids
like to play games because kids play games

00:45:43.530 --> 00:45:49.040
because games are a simplified analog of life,
and if you get good at playing games - well

00:45:49.040 --> 00:45:53.250
what happens is that the games you play are
increasingly complex and more and more real

00:45:53.250 --> 00:45:57.710
world like until they're actually indistinguishable
from acting in the world.

00:45:57.710 --> 00:46:05.920
But they're even then they're still games
in a sense because they could - the rules

00:46:05.920 --> 00:46:06.920
could be different.

00:46:06.920 --> 00:46:09.590
But then, you might think well are there things
that couldn’t be different?

00:46:09.590 --> 00:46:10.890
Well, yeah there are some things.

00:46:10.890 --> 00:46:12.170
It’s gonna be cooperative.

00:46:12.170 --> 00:46:13.579
It’s gonna be competitive.

00:46:13.579 --> 00:46:15.540
There's gonna be rules.

00:46:15.540 --> 00:46:17.540
Everybody needs to agree on the rules.

00:46:17.540 --> 00:46:18.730
People have to want to play.

00:46:18.730 --> 00:46:20.520
This is something else Piaget observed.

00:46:20.520 --> 00:46:21.970
It’s bloody brilliant.

00:46:21.970 --> 00:46:28.750
He thought well, how can you decide if one
game is better than another.

00:46:28.750 --> 00:46:33.640
Let’s say that's a moral claim in a sense,
but he wanted to narrow it down so that it

00:46:33.640 --> 00:46:35.800
was possible it could be answered.

00:46:35.800 --> 00:46:40.950
What he said was imagine you have group A
that was attempting to obtain a goal, and

00:46:40.950 --> 00:46:43.119
group B that are attempting to attain a goal.

00:46:43.119 --> 00:46:47.650
Both groups have agreed that attaining the
goal is valuable.

00:46:47.650 --> 00:46:50.089
We don’t have to argue about the value of
the goal.

00:46:50.089 --> 00:46:51.390
We’ve settled that.

00:46:51.390 --> 00:46:55.960
Okay now, we’re going to make a further
assumption which is all things considered,

00:46:55.960 --> 00:46:59.970
getting towards the goal with less effort,
more enjoyment, and less trouble would be

00:46:59.970 --> 00:47:00.970
better.

00:47:00.970 --> 00:47:04.220
Okay, you have to take that as a given because
it might not be.

00:47:04.220 --> 00:47:06.349
Sometimes something difficult is better.

00:47:06.349 --> 00:47:13.630
Well then you might say okay, would people
working voluntarily or involuntarily be more

00:47:13.630 --> 00:47:19.230
likely be able to reach that goal with less
misery faster and more effectively.

00:47:19.230 --> 00:47:23.930
Piaget’s answer was if you could get people
to play the game voluntarily, you don't have

00:47:23.930 --> 00:47:29.829
to waste effort on enforcement, no one kicks
back so the system is going to be more efficient

00:47:29.829 --> 00:47:31.050
across time.

00:47:31.050 --> 00:47:38.609
Now that’s smart because he’s got a way
of conceptualizing what might constitute a

00:47:38.609 --> 00:47:40.130
better system.

00:47:40.130 --> 00:47:43.640
Now it’s grounded in subjective experience
in some degree.

00:47:43.640 --> 00:47:48.820
Less misery, more enjoyment, but there's also
a way of measuring it.

00:47:48.820 --> 00:47:54.810
It’s also more efficient, or it does what
it’s supposed to do with less noise and

00:47:54.810 --> 00:47:55.810
grief.

00:47:55.810 --> 00:47:56.960
Less cost, let’s say.

00:47:56.960 --> 00:47:59.520
So that’s pretty smart.

00:47:59.520 --> 00:48:07.540
Okay, so then let’s make another - well
how old do you think kids games are?

00:48:07.540 --> 00:48:12.110
And I don’t know what’s happened to kids
in recent years because their culture has

00:48:12.110 --> 00:48:17.980
somewhat been seriously disrupted by television
and computer, but you guys are a lot younger

00:48:17.980 --> 00:48:18.980
than me.

00:48:18.980 --> 00:48:21.020
Did you play hide and go seek?

00:48:21.020 --> 00:48:23.170
You know ring around the rosie?

00:48:23.170 --> 00:48:24.170
You know that.

00:48:24.170 --> 00:48:25.170
Do you know what that is?

00:48:25.170 --> 00:48:26.170
You don’t know Ring Around the Rosie.

00:48:26.170 --> 00:48:27.829
How many people don’t know that.

00:48:27.829 --> 00:48:31.560
Ring around the rosie, pockets full of posies,
hush ah hush ah, we all fall down?

00:48:31.560 --> 00:48:33.360
Okay, how many know that.

00:48:33.360 --> 00:48:35.050
It’s an English nursery rhyme.

00:48:35.050 --> 00:48:36.400
It’s about the plague.

00:48:36.400 --> 00:48:39.250
It’s about the black plague by the way.

00:48:39.250 --> 00:48:44.200
And so, that piece of doggerel, that piece
of rhyme has been around, transmitted by the

00:48:44.200 --> 00:48:47.190
oral culture of children since the time of
the black plague.

00:48:47.190 --> 00:48:52.700
And so a lot of children’s tradition is
in the oral tradition, and a lot of the games

00:48:52.700 --> 00:48:56.339
children play are passed along by children.

00:48:56.339 --> 00:48:58.460
Kids learn how to play hide and go seek.

00:48:58.460 --> 00:49:02.480
And how many of you played tag?

00:49:02.480 --> 00:49:03.880
Anyone never play tag?

00:49:03.880 --> 00:49:06.720
Right, so everyone plays tag.

00:49:06.720 --> 00:49:10.050
How would you conceptualize tag if you were
a biologist?

00:49:10.050 --> 00:49:13.460
What do you think children are doing?

00:49:13.460 --> 00:49:16.090
They’re hunting.

00:49:16.090 --> 00:49:18.020
They're chasing things down, obviously.

00:49:18.020 --> 00:49:19.390
They're chasing things down.

00:49:19.390 --> 00:49:23.160
Now hide and go seek, that’s a tougher one,
but it’s certainly exploration.

00:49:23.160 --> 00:49:27.530
They’re exploring the landscape, and they're
matching wits with one another, but - and

00:49:27.530 --> 00:49:30.960
they're also trying to see if - you know,
do people like you enough to actually find

00:49:30.960 --> 00:49:35.440
you, which is a helpful thing to know if you're
four years old.

00:49:35.440 --> 00:49:41.940
So anyways, children pass these games down
across the centuries.

00:49:41.940 --> 00:49:45.310
Maybe tag is one of those games you don't
even have to pass down.

00:49:45.310 --> 00:49:49.540
Children might just invent it spontaneously
with every generation because you can more

00:49:49.540 --> 00:49:52.130
or less play tag with a dog.

00:49:52.130 --> 00:49:58.030
I mean, the dog doesn't quite get it, but
it’s close.

00:49:58.030 --> 00:50:04.819
Okay, so there are some games that are enjoyable
enough that they're either transmitted and

00:50:04.819 --> 00:50:09.980
everyone knows them very rapidly, or they're
so close to our nature we can just reinvent

00:50:09.980 --> 00:50:13.680
them and I think tag is the classic example
of that.

00:50:13.680 --> 00:50:18.079
So that’s interesting too because what it
means is that there’s certain types of competitive

00:50:18.079 --> 00:50:20.030
and cooperative behavior.

00:50:20.030 --> 00:50:25.820
Those are elementary moral systems that are
- they look like they're integral to our nature.

00:50:25.820 --> 00:50:30.319
You can even say they're built right in, or
you might say they're so close to built in

00:50:30.319 --> 00:50:35.510
that it doesn't take much of an introduction
for the roles of the game before everybody’s

00:50:35.510 --> 00:50:36.780
pretty happy with it.

00:50:36.780 --> 00:50:38.710
You see this with a lot of things.

00:50:38.710 --> 00:50:41.220
You play peek-a-boo with a baby.

00:50:41.220 --> 00:50:42.480
Okay so what are the rules?

00:50:42.480 --> 00:50:44.180
What are the rules of peek-a-boo.

00:50:44.180 --> 00:50:49.359
You cover your eyes.

00:50:49.359 --> 00:50:50.460
You cover your eyes.

00:50:50.460 --> 00:50:51.559
Okay so that’s right.

00:50:51.559 --> 00:50:53.290
You go like this.

00:50:53.290 --> 00:50:55.280
Okay then what’s the next rule?

00:50:55.280 --> 00:50:57.470
That’s right.

00:50:57.470 --> 00:50:58.470
You reveal your eyes.

00:50:58.470 --> 00:51:00.290
You don't just sit there like this.

00:51:00.290 --> 00:51:05.470
Now you can play a trick on a baby if you
want, and babies will laugh about this sort

00:51:05.470 --> 00:51:07.820
of thing which is really, I think, quite amazing.

00:51:07.820 --> 00:51:10.700
A baby’s laugh is something remarkable to
me.

00:51:10.700 --> 00:51:15.580
So you can go like this, and then like this
and then like this and then like this and

00:51:15.580 --> 00:51:20.710
then you can do this, and the baby will laugh
because that's a little variation eh.

00:51:20.710 --> 00:51:24.140
Or you can go like this.

00:51:24.140 --> 00:51:25.869
Right.

00:51:25.869 --> 00:51:28.570
And then the baby - well that’ll even make
you guys laugh.

00:51:28.570 --> 00:51:32.200
That’s good, you can see how deeply rooted
this is.

00:51:32.200 --> 00:51:34.640
So you know but, eventually what’ll the
baby do?

00:51:34.640 --> 00:51:38.510
It’ll grab your hands, pull them down because
you're breaking the damn rules.

00:51:38.510 --> 00:51:43.130
And the baby knows the rules even though you’ve
never established any rules, and you can get

00:51:43.130 --> 00:51:45.099
the rules going with about five reps.

00:51:45.099 --> 00:51:46.369
You say okay, this.

00:51:46.369 --> 00:51:49.369
Maybe no one’s ever played peek-a-boo with
a baby.

00:51:49.369 --> 00:51:50.809
The baby figures it out right away.

00:51:50.809 --> 00:51:53.670
This, this, and then you vary that.

00:51:53.670 --> 00:51:54.670
Maybe do that.

00:51:54.670 --> 00:51:58.580
That baby thinks that’s a hell of a good
joke, you know, cause you went up instead

00:51:58.580 --> 00:52:02.210
of down, and so it’s variations within this
rule bound theme.

00:52:02.210 --> 00:52:05.900
It’s kind of musical in a sense, there’s
variations in the theme.

00:52:05.900 --> 00:52:07.740
Now what you don’t do is go.

00:52:07.740 --> 00:52:08.740
Waahh!

00:52:08.740 --> 00:52:13.140
Cause that’ll just short it right out.

00:52:13.140 --> 00:52:20.400
So you don't do that unless you’re, you
know, unless you have an evil streak in you.

00:52:20.400 --> 00:52:24.450
So there's certainly ways of violating the
baby’s expectations and what the baby will

00:52:24.450 --> 00:52:28.619
do if you do that is startle and they startle
with their whole bodies, and then look at

00:52:28.619 --> 00:52:31.859
you and probably they’ll burst into tears.

00:52:31.859 --> 00:52:35.680
But some babies will just laugh like mad if
something like that happens, and you can find

00:52:35.680 --> 00:52:40.421
those babies all over YouTube, right, because
you get the baby that the mother sneezes and

00:52:40.421 --> 00:52:43.310
the baby has a fit laughing for like ten minutes.

00:52:43.310 --> 00:52:45.510
But it’s quite interesting.

00:52:45.510 --> 00:52:48.540
One of the things about babies that I’ve
never been able to understand is the staggering

00:52:48.540 --> 00:52:51.820
sophistication of their senses of humor.

00:52:51.820 --> 00:52:53.170
Like it’s deep eh?

00:52:53.170 --> 00:52:55.751
Cause even a nine month old baby can play
tricks on you.

00:52:55.751 --> 00:53:00.960
So, you know they're baby tricks, but still,
they’re tricks and they laugh and they're

00:53:00.960 --> 00:53:02.980
quite comical and they like variation.

00:53:02.980 --> 00:53:09.120
Anyways, the point is - one of the points
of all this is this understanding of game

00:53:09.120 --> 00:53:15.280
like structure is really really really really
really really deep inside of us, and it’s

00:53:15.280 --> 00:53:17.809
way before articulation.

00:53:17.809 --> 00:53:21.579
It’s way before we can say the rules.

00:53:21.579 --> 00:53:26.839
Babies can pick up the damn rules right away
and it’s kind of a dance in some sense,

00:53:26.839 --> 00:53:29.730
and a dance is also a rule bound structure.

00:53:29.730 --> 00:53:34.201
If you’re dancing with someone well, there’s
rules, but don't step on their feet but you

00:53:34.201 --> 00:53:39.320
also set up a set of expectations in the dance
and you violate them in tandem.

00:53:39.320 --> 00:53:42.140
And if you can do it gracefully, it’s fun.

00:53:42.140 --> 00:53:46.820
Maybe the person you’re dancing with is
an absolute klutz and so autistic you can’t

00:53:46.820 --> 00:53:51.089
even get outside of yourself, and might make
a half reasonable partner or if it’s just

00:53:51.089 --> 00:53:52.770
hopeless.

00:53:52.770 --> 00:53:54.150
But the dance is the same thing.

00:53:54.150 --> 00:53:55.910
It’s not negotiated verbally.

00:53:55.910 --> 00:54:01.839
It’s negotiated bodily, and unless we’re
able to imitate, and imitate insanely well

00:54:01.839 --> 00:54:05.970
because human beings can imitate like mad,
because we won’t be able to do that.

00:54:05.970 --> 00:54:07.550
So babies are the same way.

00:54:07.550 --> 00:54:10.430
You basically start - you don’t actually.

00:54:10.430 --> 00:54:17.070
Peek-a-boo doesn’t have rules because a
rule is a stated description of a set of expectations.

00:54:17.070 --> 00:54:23.819
What peek-a-boo has is expectations and repetition,
and you can violate the expectation but that

00:54:23.819 --> 00:54:29.400
doesn’t mean you’re violating a rule because
an expectation is not a rule until you observe

00:54:29.400 --> 00:54:34.490
the expectation, you describe what it is and
maybe even write it down.

00:54:34.490 --> 00:54:40.410
You can play games long before you can follow
rules, and one of the things Piaget observed

00:54:40.410 --> 00:54:46.350
is if you watched kids play a complicated
game like marbles and they're like six, and

00:54:46.350 --> 00:54:49.730
you take them out of the game and ask them
what the rules are, they can't tell you or

00:54:49.730 --> 00:54:52.680
they might get some of them right and a bunch
of them wrong.

00:54:52.680 --> 00:54:57.030
Maybe if you interviewed every child that
was playing, and you looked at the commonality

00:54:57.030 --> 00:55:01.319
of the descriptions, you could extract out
the underlying rule structure of the game.

00:55:01.319 --> 00:55:03.160
But the kids don't know the rules.

00:55:03.160 --> 00:55:08.420
What they know is how to play with other kids
when they're playing marbles, and a lot of

00:55:08.420 --> 00:55:13.569
that’s nonverbal hints and eyebrow raises
and no you can’t do that and so forth.

00:55:13.569 --> 00:55:16.819
It isn't much later until they can tell you
the rules, if ever.

00:55:16.819 --> 00:55:22.780
So like, you know, you guys can't tell me
the rules for dancing successfully with someone.

00:55:22.780 --> 00:55:27.569
You can tell me some stupid things you shouldn’t
do, but imagine how complex it is.

00:55:27.569 --> 00:55:31.650
It’s very difficult to articulate out the
whole structure, but you can sure tell when

00:55:31.650 --> 00:55:36.040
it’s happening, and you can sure tell when
you’re not doing it or when your partner

00:55:36.040 --> 00:55:37.040
isn’t.

00:55:37.040 --> 00:55:41.540
So partly what that also means is you know
how to do things you don't know how to do,

00:55:41.540 --> 00:55:45.900
and you know how to all sorts of things you
don't know how you know how to do.

00:55:45.900 --> 00:55:48.619
In fact, most of what you know is like that.

00:55:48.619 --> 00:55:55.910
That also means we can be playing games that
are extraordinarily sophisticated that we

00:55:55.910 --> 00:55:59.960
don’t understand, but that doesn't mean
- well we can't describe at least.

00:55:59.960 --> 00:56:04.960
We don't have an articulated knowledge of
the structure, but we can still play them.

00:56:04.960 --> 00:56:07.910
Now, I don't know if you know this or not.

00:56:07.910 --> 00:56:11.690
You might know it.

00:56:11.690 --> 00:56:16.290
A while back, twenty years ago - about that.

00:56:16.290 --> 00:56:23.859
Those massive online multiplayer games started
to become popular, and some of them - I think,

00:56:23.859 --> 00:56:27.280
although I don't know this for sure - some
of them lasted a long time, and maybe some

00:56:27.280 --> 00:56:29.560
of them still being played, like the early
ones.

00:56:29.560 --> 00:56:35.780
So they developed entire worlds in there and
you know, there was an economist who became

00:56:35.780 --> 00:56:38.079
quite famous because of those games.

00:56:38.079 --> 00:56:40.410
Castronova, I think his name was.

00:56:40.410 --> 00:56:41.590
Edward Castronova.

00:56:41.590 --> 00:56:45.630
I think that’s right, but it’s close.

00:56:45.630 --> 00:56:50.160
Anyways, he thought hey look man those aren’t
games, those are countries.

00:56:50.160 --> 00:56:56.299
So one of the things he tried to do was calculate
their gross national product, and you know,

00:56:56.299 --> 00:57:00.740
you can trade items in those little worlds
and you could sell them on eBay.

00:57:00.740 --> 00:57:05.569
For awhile in this one game, and maybe this
still happens, there were young people in

00:57:05.569 --> 00:57:09.720
the Philippines who were working inside these
games making artifacts that they were selling

00:57:09.720 --> 00:57:14.670
on eBay to other game players because they
could make more money doing that than actually

00:57:14.670 --> 00:57:18.000
working in the actual world.

00:57:18.000 --> 00:57:22.340
So Castronova - I think was his name - said
well you don't get it.

00:57:22.340 --> 00:57:23.660
That is the real world.

00:57:23.660 --> 00:57:28.210
It’s not a game, or if it’s a game it’s
so much like the world that it’s part of

00:57:28.210 --> 00:57:29.210
the world.

00:57:29.210 --> 00:57:32.980
So he calculated the gross national product
of some of these massive games, and they were

00:57:32.980 --> 00:57:35.809
like the 30th richest country in the world.

00:57:35.809 --> 00:57:37.940
It was insane.

00:57:37.940 --> 00:57:43.720
So that’s interesting because that also
shows you that the line between a game and

00:57:43.720 --> 00:57:46.140
reality is not clear.

00:57:46.140 --> 00:57:48.650
Here’s another example of that.

00:57:48.650 --> 00:57:51.849
Something worth thinking about.

00:57:51.849 --> 00:57:57.171
Some of you have no doubt played very complex
multiplayer online games, and I suppose those

00:57:57.171 --> 00:58:00.890
are often adventure games or quest games and
you have to communicate with other people

00:58:00.890 --> 00:58:06.170
and you have to solve problems and maybe you
have to defend yourselves, like you're in

00:58:06.170 --> 00:58:08.170
an exploratory party in a new world.

00:58:08.170 --> 00:58:09.170
Something like that.

00:58:09.170 --> 00:58:14.839
So it’s pretty sophisticated, and okay some
of you have worked at McDonald’s or another

00:58:14.839 --> 00:58:15.839
fast food restaurant.

00:58:15.839 --> 00:58:18.400
How many of you have worked at a restaurant.

00:58:18.400 --> 00:58:20.630
How many at a fast food restaurant.

00:58:20.630 --> 00:58:26.809
Would you say that an online game is more
like the world, or less like the world than

00:58:26.809 --> 00:58:29.160
your job at a fast food restaurant.

00:58:29.160 --> 00:58:30.160
More.

00:58:30.160 --> 00:58:31.990
It’s more.

00:58:31.990 --> 00:58:33.869
Anybody disagree?

00:58:33.869 --> 00:58:36.900
Okay, why is it more like the real world?

00:58:36.900 --> 00:58:41.110
Because you’re doing more complex stuff.

00:58:41.110 --> 00:58:42.510
What are you doing?

00:58:42.510 --> 00:58:43.510
You’re exploring.

00:58:43.510 --> 00:58:44.510
You’re thinking.

00:58:44.510 --> 00:58:49.120
You’re getting out of situations because
new ones are always coming up and you're dealing

00:58:49.120 --> 00:58:50.869
with them and you’re learning.

00:58:50.869 --> 00:58:55.830
Fast food is like you're doing - Right, you’re
a real cog in a fast food machine.

00:58:55.830 --> 00:58:57.720
Yeah, but it’s in the real world.

00:58:57.720 --> 00:58:58.720
Is it a game?

00:58:58.720 --> 00:59:01.319
You’re more like a machine in the fast food
part.

00:59:01.319 --> 00:59:09.740
So does that mean McDonald’s is less real
than a multiplayer online game?

00:59:09.740 --> 00:59:13.930
Well it’s a tough one, eh, because when
you’re trying to think about what real means,

00:59:13.930 --> 00:59:16.780
is you might think maybe I phrased that wrongly.

00:59:16.780 --> 00:59:23.589
Maybe I could say is McDonald’s a worse
model of the real world than a multiplayer

00:59:23.589 --> 00:59:25.200
online game?

00:59:25.200 --> 00:59:29.140
And it’s hard because it’s hard to say
McDonald’s isn’t part of the real world

00:59:29.140 --> 00:59:31.859
so a priori, the claim is kind of absurd.

00:59:31.859 --> 00:59:34.869
It’s like well, you’re in a building.

00:59:34.869 --> 00:59:35.930
People actually come there.

00:59:35.930 --> 00:59:37.049
They give you money.

00:59:37.049 --> 00:59:38.460
It’s real.

00:59:38.460 --> 00:59:43.790
But then, well you think about these complex
games and you’re practicing an extraordinarily

00:59:43.790 --> 00:59:48.040
sophisticated form of social and intellectual
behavior and we actually don’t know what

00:59:48.040 --> 00:59:49.070
that does to people.

00:59:49.070 --> 00:59:51.980
Maybe it makes them extraordinarily sophisticated
and social.

00:59:51.980 --> 00:59:57.680
I do think it helps with introverts quite
a lot because it’s harder for introverts

00:59:57.680 --> 01:00:00.849
to become extremely socially fluid because
people wear them out.

01:00:00.849 --> 01:00:05.940
A lot of introverts do a lot of heavy duty
socializing on the net, and in my experience

01:00:05.940 --> 01:00:13.500
it’s been, all things considered, probably
a plus rather than a minus.

01:00:13.500 --> 01:00:19.710
With regards to your use of computational
devices, one of the things I used to see my

01:00:19.710 --> 01:00:22.480
kids doing is they would sit together in a
group.

01:00:22.480 --> 01:00:27.580
They were teenagers, and there’d be a bunch
of friends that weren't there and while they

01:00:27.580 --> 01:00:30.650
were interacting with all their friends who
were there, they were also interacting with

01:00:30.650 --> 01:00:32.109
all their friends who weren’t there.

01:00:32.109 --> 01:00:35.300
Is that a common thing for teenagers to do?

01:00:35.300 --> 01:00:37.750
You’re texting your friends and so forth.

01:00:37.750 --> 01:00:40.250
Is that reasonably standard behavior?

01:00:40.250 --> 01:00:42.260
How many of you have done that?

01:00:42.260 --> 01:00:44.770
Okay how many of you have done that regularly?

01:00:44.770 --> 01:00:46.960
Okay, how many of you haven’t done that?

01:00:46.960 --> 01:00:50.369
Okay, so most people have done it but not
everyone.

01:00:50.369 --> 01:00:55.560
For those of you have done it, is that more
advanced or less advanced social behavior?

01:00:55.560 --> 01:00:56.650
More.

01:00:56.650 --> 01:00:57.680
Okay you think more.

01:00:57.680 --> 01:00:58.680
Why?

01:00:58.680 --> 01:01:01.690
Cause you’re keeping your friends in the
room while they’re not there.

01:01:01.690 --> 01:01:04.270
So it’s like they have a conceptual idea
of what you’re doing and it’s like - they

01:01:04.270 --> 01:01:15.980
understand where the group dynamics are moving
towards without them actually having be there.

01:01:15.980 --> 01:01:22.530
Okay, do you think that you paid a price for
it in the decrease of the interactions you

01:01:22.530 --> 01:01:25.119
were having with the people that were actually
there?

01:01:25.119 --> 01:01:26.119
Not really.Yes.

01:01:26.119 --> 01:01:27.119
You think so.

01:01:27.119 --> 01:01:30.820
Okay, how many people think they do pay a
price for it?

01:01:30.820 --> 01:01:33.420
How many people think they didn’t?

01:01:33.420 --> 01:01:39.010
Okay, so - well let’s start with you guys
then.

01:01:39.010 --> 01:01:40.980
Why did you think you didn't pay a price for
it?

01:01:40.980 --> 01:01:41.980
Well because I feel like it really doesn't
take that much time to just text and go back

01:01:41.980 --> 01:01:42.980
to talk to your friends.

01:01:42.980 --> 01:01:43.980
Okay.

01:01:43.980 --> 01:01:47.730
Well I think that you can pay a price, but
if you’re doing it correctly and you’re

01:01:47.730 --> 01:01:59.390
monitoring the conversation you're taking
a part of, you can see when it would be appropriate

01:01:59.390 --> 01:02:08.069
to text someone else which probably means
you're paying more attention to the people

01:02:08.069 --> 01:02:11.799
you're talking with because you're also texting.

01:02:11.799 --> 01:02:18.200
Okay so you’re kind of taking a multitasking
rationale for it in some sense, although assuming

01:02:18.200 --> 01:02:20.619
you have to also heighten your attention to
manage that.

01:02:20.619 --> 01:02:24.240
There’s people who didn't think - that you
did pay a price for it.

01:02:24.240 --> 01:02:25.520
Who thought that?

01:02:25.520 --> 01:02:26.930
Okay, what sort of price?

01:02:26.930 --> 01:02:31.760
I think that especially when you're texting
people, there's so much complexity that gets

01:02:31.760 --> 01:02:36.580
lost in translation, like you don't really
convey your feelings, like there's so much

01:02:36.580 --> 01:02:38.310
more to use than language.

01:02:38.310 --> 01:02:39.800
Okay then they certainly lose something.

01:02:39.800 --> 01:02:47.049
What about you, while you're interacting with
your actual present friends?

01:02:47.049 --> 01:03:02.890
I would say you have to commit some certain
amount of effort to writing a text, you ignore

01:03:02.890 --> 01:03:03.990
aspects of - for example, they’re asking
about something and then you go and text the

01:03:03.990 --> 01:03:07.270
person and you come back and miss some part
of the conversation.

01:03:07.270 --> 01:03:08.270
Yeah, so it’s like kind of -
Yeah so it’s kind of like start, stop, start,

01:03:08.270 --> 01:03:09.270
stop.

01:03:09.270 --> 01:03:10.270
I don't think you can one hundred percent
commit yourself to -

01:03:10.270 --> 01:03:11.370
Well one of the things you suggested was that
some of that can be mitigated by doing it

01:03:11.370 --> 01:03:17.940
properly, which implies there's an emergent
norm for doing this sort of thing and that

01:03:17.940 --> 01:03:22.740
if you're good at complying with the norm,
then you’re not irritating people and maybe

01:03:22.740 --> 01:03:24.210
you can handle both things at once.

01:03:24.210 --> 01:03:25.210
Something like that.

01:03:25.210 --> 01:03:30.470
But if it’s properly to the people who are
there, is it proper for the friend who is

01:03:30.470 --> 01:03:32.090
waiting for you to answer?

01:03:32.090 --> 01:03:36.270
Well I guess one answer to that is that’s
up to them to determine, like if they don't

01:03:36.270 --> 01:03:39.190
want to be part of it, they can do something
else.

01:03:39.190 --> 01:03:42.890
The reason I’m saying that specifically
is that you can imagine that’s how the norm

01:03:42.890 --> 01:03:43.890
develops.

01:03:43.890 --> 01:03:50.170
It’s like obviously if a successful norm
is going to develop that allows text conversation

01:03:50.170 --> 01:03:56.289
and real world conversation to occur at the
same time, everybody has to want it to happen.

01:03:56.289 --> 01:04:01.330
That means if you can’t do it in the manner
that everyone wants, it’s going to die out.

01:04:01.330 --> 01:04:09.369
Now see, that's another example of how you
can see how moral structures - I think - might

01:04:09.369 --> 01:04:13.560
evolve, is the right way of thinking about
it because I think moral structures evolve.

01:04:13.560 --> 01:04:17.240
And I think they’re just as real as anything
else that evolves.

01:04:17.240 --> 01:04:20.690
Now, evolved things can be staggeringly complex.

01:04:20.690 --> 01:04:25.349
Just think about how many lifeforms there
are, and how many niches there are for their

01:04:25.349 --> 01:04:30.220
lifeforms and so you have in some sense an
infinite variety, but it’s not infinite,

01:04:30.220 --> 01:04:32.289
and it follows patterns.

01:04:32.289 --> 01:04:36.500
Not just any old thing can be alive, and not
just any old thing can be social.

01:04:36.500 --> 01:04:42.630
Here’s an example, and I found this out
awhile back.

01:04:42.630 --> 01:04:48.920
I did a lot of neurobiological research.

01:04:48.920 --> 01:04:55.030
Some of it hands on, a lot of it conceptual
especially in the early days of my graduate

01:04:55.030 --> 01:04:56.030
work.

01:04:56.030 --> 01:04:58.990
I got really interested in the function of
serotonin.

01:04:58.990 --> 01:05:03.510
You all know, undoubtedly, that one of the
things antidepressants do is decrease the

01:05:03.510 --> 01:05:06.951
rate at which serotonin is pulled back up
into the neurons that deliver it and that

01:05:06.951 --> 01:05:09.790
seems to help stabilize people’s negative
emotions.

01:05:09.790 --> 01:05:14.420
Makes them less volatile, less anxious, less
likely to withdraw, and so - or you could

01:05:14.420 --> 01:05:17.010
say it makes them more confident.

01:05:17.010 --> 01:05:24.109
Now we also know if you say we look at a chimp
hierarchy, you take bottom chimp and you give

01:05:24.109 --> 01:05:28.320
him anti-depressants, he looks a lot more
like top chimp.

01:05:28.320 --> 01:05:32.160
So, because the bottom chimp world is a very
dangerous place.

01:05:32.160 --> 01:05:33.160
It’s very uncertain.

01:05:33.160 --> 01:05:34.960
He doesn't have a place to sleep.

01:05:34.960 --> 01:05:36.720
He doesn't have high quality mates.

01:05:36.720 --> 01:05:38.070
He doesn't have good things to eat.

01:05:38.070 --> 01:05:40.400
He’s stressed man, it’s bad down there.

01:05:40.400 --> 01:05:44.200
So maybe that's part of the reason his nervous
system is tuned so that he’s more anxious

01:05:44.200 --> 01:05:47.980
and upset by everything because everything
is more dangerous.

01:05:47.980 --> 01:05:49.710
Part of that’s modulated by serotonin.

01:05:49.710 --> 01:05:51.980
Okay, so here’s a rough rule.

01:05:51.980 --> 01:05:59.430
If there’s a hierarchy in an animal kingdom,
as you move up the hierarchy, your serotonin

01:05:59.430 --> 01:06:04.079
levels increase and as you move down the hierarchy,
your serotonin levels decrease and that’s

01:06:04.079 --> 01:06:08.339
somewhat independent of your absolute wealth.

01:06:08.339 --> 01:06:09.380
So here’s a famous experiment.

01:06:09.380 --> 01:06:13.589
It’s called the White Hall experiment and
it was done on English bureaucrats, and the

01:06:13.589 --> 01:06:17.040
thing that’s so cool about the White Hall
experiments is they were done fifty years

01:06:17.040 --> 01:06:18.040
apart.

01:06:18.040 --> 01:06:23.280
So what they showed basically was - imagine
you take an English bureaucracy and you stratify

01:06:23.280 --> 01:06:24.770
it by position.

01:06:24.770 --> 01:06:29.310
Englishmen are hierarchical, and so you stratify
it by position.

01:06:29.310 --> 01:06:35.110
You have the top people and the bottom people
and what you find is the bottom people die

01:06:35.110 --> 01:06:37.440
faster and more often.

01:06:37.440 --> 01:06:41.400
You might say if you're not that healthy,
you end up at the bottom.

01:06:41.400 --> 01:06:46.490
Fair enough, but there seems to be an effect
above the effect of just general health.

01:06:46.490 --> 01:06:49.520
It’s worse to be at the bottom than at the
top.

01:06:49.520 --> 01:06:55.890
Okay, that was true 70 years ago, and another
study was done 50 years after that.

01:06:55.890 --> 01:07:01.480
Now the thing that’s so cool is by that
time, from a material perspective, the people

01:07:01.480 --> 01:07:06.980
at the bottom of the modern hierarchy were
at least as wealthy as the people who were

01:07:06.980 --> 01:07:16.230
like two thirds of the way up 50 years before
because everybody had running water, everyone

01:07:16.230 --> 01:07:18.660
had enough heat for the winter, you know.

01:07:18.660 --> 01:07:20.200
Everyone had shelter, and so on.

01:07:20.200 --> 01:07:27.530
So you figure, with regards to wealth, once
you’ve got running water, reliable shelter,

01:07:27.530 --> 01:07:33.520
heat, and maybe air conditioning if you live
in the desert, and you’re not hungry, you’ve

01:07:33.520 --> 01:07:36.079
got ninety percent of what wealth can deliver.

01:07:36.079 --> 01:07:37.910
Really, all things considered.

01:07:37.910 --> 01:07:46.329
The rest of it is just status and luxury items,
but the relationship between position of status

01:07:46.329 --> 01:07:50.500
and death rates remain constant.

01:07:50.500 --> 01:07:54.710
So that’s very interesting, and we know
that that’s true from a lot of other studies

01:07:54.710 --> 01:07:55.710
as well.

01:07:55.710 --> 01:08:00.450
It’s like control for absolute wealth, there’s
still problems with hierarchy, and that seems

01:08:00.450 --> 01:08:05.020
mediated at least in part by serotonin, and
serotonin regulates negative emotion, and

01:08:05.020 --> 01:08:08.819
the more negative emotion that you experience,
the more cortisol you produce, and cortisol

01:08:08.819 --> 01:08:13.640
in high levels is toxic and suppresses your
immune system so your brain gets damaged,

01:08:13.640 --> 01:08:17.309
and you gain weight and you get diabetes,
and maybe develop Alzheimer’s disease and

01:08:17.309 --> 01:08:22.130
are maybe more prone to cancer and heart disease,
and that’s just not good.

01:08:22.130 --> 01:08:32.779
Now that serotonergic mechanism is really
old, and so you can give serotonin injections

01:08:32.779 --> 01:08:36.989
to unhappy lobsters and it cheers them right
up, at least behaviorally speaking.

01:08:36.989 --> 01:08:40.911
If they're defeated in a fight, and you give
them a serotonin injection, they’ll stand

01:08:40.911 --> 01:08:43.710
up tall, confident and they’ll fight again.

01:08:43.710 --> 01:08:48.339
I’m telling you that for a very specific
reason.

01:08:48.339 --> 01:08:53.109
One of the things we’ve been asking about
is well how might you conceptualize moral

01:08:53.109 --> 01:08:55.880
systems and how might you conceptualize evolution?

01:08:55.880 --> 01:09:03.650
One of the propositions was there are playable
games and non-playable games.

01:09:03.650 --> 01:09:05.270
Well here’s a playable game.

01:09:05.270 --> 01:09:06.520
Dominance hierarchy.

01:09:06.520 --> 01:09:10.580
We’ve been playing it for four hundred million
years.

01:09:10.580 --> 01:09:12.290
It’s stable.

01:09:12.290 --> 01:09:19.130
Now, it’s so stable that even lobsters have
them, but even worse is that the biochemical

01:09:19.130 --> 01:09:28.330
systems the lobsters use act within the dominance
hierarchy and react to, it’s the same in

01:09:28.330 --> 01:09:29.390
us.

01:09:29.390 --> 01:09:36.359
And so, not only is that stable, it’s well
ridiculously stable.

01:09:36.359 --> 01:09:43.620
So one of the things we could say is that
one of the games that seems emergent and real

01:09:43.620 --> 01:09:44.850
is a dominance hierarchy.

01:09:44.850 --> 01:09:49.980
And then we might ask what exactly constitutes
a dominance hierarchy, and we might ask well

01:09:49.980 --> 01:09:55.409
what constitutes a highly functional hierarchy
versus a dysfunctional hierarchy, and even

01:09:55.409 --> 01:09:58.880
what does highly functional and dysfunctional
mean.

01:09:58.880 --> 01:10:01.070
Well we have to define all those terms.

01:10:01.070 --> 01:10:02.840
We will, as we move forward.

01:10:02.840 --> 01:10:08.560
But the point I’m making is - and this is
a bit of a contradiction with the initial

01:10:08.560 --> 01:10:14.640
system I made with the tight distinction between
the scientific world and the objective world

01:10:14.640 --> 01:10:21.510
because I’m also talking about the evolution
of games, and you know, the study of evolution

01:10:21.510 --> 01:10:28.030
is a scientific study and the dominance hierarchy
is a biological phenomena and so in some sense

01:10:28.030 --> 01:10:32.040
it’s also a game, and it’s also a moral
structure and so there are levels of analysis

01:10:32.040 --> 01:10:35.540
where these things start to get tangled together,
and that’s partly what we’ll try to sort

01:10:35.540 --> 01:10:37.040
out.

01:10:37.040 --> 01:10:42.989
So, we’ll do that in the future because
it’s very very complicated.

01:10:42.989 --> 01:10:53.590
Alright now we’re gonna step back a little
bit.

